  LS.lessons["c-what-is-finance"] = {
    id: "c-what-is-finance", minutes: 5,
    title: "What is finance?",
    short: "What is finance?",
    desc: "Finance is the study of how money moves between people who have it and people who can use it.",
    lede: "Finance is about one simple situation: some people have money they are not using right now, and other people have a use for money they do not currently have.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Finance is about one simple situation: some people have money they are not using right now, and other people have a use for money they do not currently have. Finance is the set of tools, contracts and institutions that move money from the first group to the second — and works out what the second group should pay for the privilege. A bank deposit, a home loan, a share of a company and a government bond are all versions of the same idea." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Almost every financial decision you will ever make is a version of this question: should money move, in which direction, and on what terms? Once you see that a savings account and a corporate acquisition are the same shape of problem, the rest of the curriculum stops being a list of unrelated topics." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Someone with spare money (a saver, an investor, a lender) wants more money later than they have now.<br><br>2. Someone who needs money now (a borrower, a company, a government) is willing to pay for the use of it.<br><br>3. The two sides agree terms: how much, for how long, what gets paid back, and what happens if things go wrong.<br><br>4. The price of that agreement is set by two things — how long the money is tied up, and how likely it is that it does not come back." },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>You have ₹1,00,000 sitting idle. A neighbour wants to borrow it for a year to buy equipment for her shop.</p><ol><li>If you keep the money, you have ₹1,00,000 in a year — and it will buy slightly less because prices rise.</li><li>If you lend it at 9%, you are owed ₹1,09,000 in a year.</li><li>The extra ₹9,000 is your payment for two things: waiting a year, and the chance she cannot repay.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> That ₹9,000 is not arbitrary. It is the price of time plus the price of risk. Every interest rate, every expected stock return and every valuation in this curriculum is built from those same two ingredients.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Capital", h: "Money that is being put to work rather than sitting idle." },
      { t: "def", term: "Lender / investor", h: "The side supplying the money and expecting more back later." },
      { t: "def", term: "Borrower / issuer", h: "The side receiving the money now and owing something later." },
      { t: "def", term: "Return", h: "The reward for supplying money, usually expressed as a percentage per year." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> You lend ₹50,000 to a friend's business for one year at 12%. How much are you owed at the end, and what two things is the extra amount paying you for?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">₹50,000 × 12% = ₹6,000, so you are owed ₹56,000. The ₹6,000 pays you for waiting a year and for the risk the business cannot repay.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> Name the two sides present in every financial arrangement, and say which side a fixed-deposit holder is on.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">A supplier of money and a user of money. A fixed-deposit holder supplies money — the bank uses it and pays for the privilege.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> A government bond pays 7% and a small startup loan offers 18%. Using the time-and-risk idea, explain the 11-point gap in one sentence.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Both tie money up for time, but the startup is far more likely not to repay, so lenders demand roughly 11 extra points as payment for that risk.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Finance moves money from people who have it to people who can use it.<br>• The price of that movement compensates for time and for risk.<br>• Savings accounts, loans, bonds and shares are variations on one structure, not separate subjects." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Thinking finance is mainly about the stock market. Lending, saving and company financing are far larger.<br><br>Assuming a higher promised return is simply better, without asking what risk is attached to it." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "At its core, finance is about…", opts: ["Predicting stock prices", "Moving money between those who have it and those who can use it, at a price", "Avoiding all risk", "Accounting rules"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Savings accounts, loans, bonds and shares are all mechanisms for moving money between suppliers and users, priced for time and risk.", "Correct. Savings accounts, loans, bonds and shares are all mechanisms for moving money between suppliers and users, priced for time and risk.", "Not this one. Savings accounts, loans, bonds and shares are all mechanisms for moving money between suppliers and users, priced for time and risk.", "Not this one. Savings accounts, loans, bonds and shares are all mechanisms for moving money between suppliers and users, priced for time and risk."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "You lend ₹1,00,000 at 9% for a year. The ₹9,000 compensates you for…", opts: ["Inflation only", "The lender's paperwork", "Waiting, plus the chance of not being repaid", "Nothing — it is a gift"], correct: 2, why: ["Not this one. Every return decomposes into a price for time and a price for risk. Here ₹9,000 covers a year of waiting and the repayment risk.", "Not this one. Every return decomposes into a price for time and a price for risk. Here ₹9,000 covers a year of waiting and the repayment risk.", "Correct. Every return decomposes into a price for time and a price for risk. Here ₹9,000 covers a year of waiting and the repayment risk.", "Not this one. Every return decomposes into a price for time and a price for risk. Here ₹9,000 covers a year of waiting and the repayment risk."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Which of these is NOT a supplier of capital?", opts: ["A saver with a bank deposit", "A bond buyer", "A shareholder", "A company borrowing for a new factory"], correct: 3, why: ["Not this one. The borrowing company is the user of capital. Savers, bond buyers and shareholders all supply money and expect more back later.", "Not this one. The borrowing company is the user of capital. Savers, bond buyers and shareholders all supply money and expect more back later.", "Not this one. The borrowing company is the user of capital. Savers, bond buyers and shareholders all supply money and expect more back later.", "Correct. The borrowing company is the user of capital. Savers, bond buyers and shareholders all supply money and expect more back later."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-personal-corporate-investing"] = {
    id: "c-personal-corporate-investing", minutes: 5,
    title: "Personal finance vs corporate finance vs investing",
    short: "Personal finance vs corporate finance vs investing",
    desc: "Three settings for the same decisions: yours, a company's, and an investor's.",
    lede: "Finance is usually taught in three settings.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Finance is usually taught in three settings. Personal finance is you deciding what to do with your own money — spending, saving, borrowing, insuring. Corporate finance is a company making the same decisions with shareholders' money — which projects to fund, how to pay for them, what to do with profits. Investing is choosing which of those companies, loans or assets to put money into, and at what price." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "The three settings share the same maths but ask different questions, and beginners often apply the wrong one. 'Is this a good company?' is a corporate-finance question. 'Is this a good price?' is an investing question. Confusing them is how people end up buying excellent businesses at terrible prices." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Personal finance asks: given my income and goals, how much do I spend, save, borrow and insure?<br><br>2. Corporate finance asks: which projects should this company invest in, how should it raise the money, and how much should it return to owners?<br><br>3. Investing asks: of all the available assets, which ones am I being paid enough to own?<br><br>4. All three use the same underlying tools — time value of money, risk and return — applied to a different decision-maker." },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>A coffee chain is deciding whether to open a new outlet costing ₹40 lakh.</p><ol><li>Corporate finance question: will the outlet earn more than the 12% the company's investors require?</li><li>Investing question: at today's share price, is the market already assuming the company opens 50 profitable outlets?</li><li>Personal finance question: should I put ₹50,000 of my savings into this share, or clear my 14% personal loan first?</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> Same company, three different correct answers. Clearing a 14% loan beats an uncertain 12% investment, which is why the personal answer can differ from the corporate one.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Personal finance", h: "Managing your own money: spending, saving, borrowing, insuring." },
      { t: "def", term: "Corporate finance", h: "How a company chooses investments and pays for them." },
      { t: "def", term: "Investing", h: "Deciding which assets to buy and at what price." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> You have ₹2,00,000, a personal loan at 15%, and a mutual fund expected to return 12%. Which use of the money wins, and by how much per year?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Repaying the loan saves a certain 15% = ₹30,000 a year; the fund offers an uncertain 12% = ₹24,000. Repayment wins by ₹6,000 — and by certainty.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> Classify each question: (a) Should Tata Motors build a new plant? (b) Is Tata Motors stock cheap at today's price? (c) Should I sell my Tata stock to fund a home deposit?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">(a) corporate finance, (b) investing, (c) personal finance. Same company, three different decision-makers and tools.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> A friend says 'It's a brilliant company, so buy the stock at any price.' Which two settings are being confused?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Corporate quality (a corporate-finance judgement) and investment attractiveness (which depends on price). A great company at too high a price is a poor investment.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "The same tools apply in all three settings; only the decision-maker changes.<br>• A good business and a good investment are different claims — the second depends on price.<br>• Paying off expensive debt is often the highest-certainty 'investment' available to an individual." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Treating 'this is a great company' as a complete investment argument.<br><br>Investing spare cash while carrying credit-card debt at a much higher rate." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "'Is this share worth ₹500?' is fundamentally a question of…", opts: ["Personal finance", "Corporate finance", "Investing", "Accounting"], correct: 2, why: ["Not this one. Investing asks whether an asset is attractive at a given price. Whether the company itself is well run is a separate question.", "Not this one. Investing asks whether an asset is attractive at a given price. Whether the company itself is well run is a separate question.", "Correct. Investing asks whether an asset is attractive at a given price. Whether the company itself is well run is a separate question.", "Not this one. Investing asks whether an asset is attractive at a given price. Whether the company itself is well run is a separate question."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "You carry a credit-card balance at 36% and have spare cash. The soundest first move is usually…", opts: ["A stock expected to return 14%", "Repaying the card", "A fixed deposit at 7%", "Keeping cash idle"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Repayment is a guaranteed 36% saving. Almost no investment offers a certain return anywhere near that.", "Correct. Repayment is a guaranteed 36% saving. Almost no investment offers a certain return anywhere near that.", "Not this one. Repayment is a guaranteed 36% saving. Almost no investment offers a certain return anywhere near that.", "Not this one. Repayment is a guaranteed 36% saving. Almost no investment offers a certain return anywhere near that."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Corporate finance primarily decides…", opts: ["Which stocks households should buy", "Which projects a company funds and how it raises the money", "Government tax policy", "Exchange trading hours"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Corporate finance is the company's own investment and funding decisions, made with shareholders' capital.", "Correct. Corporate finance is the company's own investment and funding decisions, made with shareholders' capital.", "Not this one. Corporate finance is the company's own investment and funding decisions, made with shareholders' capital.", "Not this one. Corporate finance is the company's own investment and funding decisions, made with shareholders' capital."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-financial-markets"] = {
    id: "c-financial-markets", minutes: 5,
    title: "Financial markets",
    short: "Financial markets",
    desc: "The places where people who want money and people who have money meet and agree a price.",
    lede: "A financial market is any organised place where financial claims are bought and sold.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "A financial market is any organised place where financial claims are bought and sold. A claim is simply a promise about future money: a share promises a slice of a company's future profits, a bond promises interest and repayment. The market's job is to let people trade those promises and, in doing so, produce a price for them." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Prices are the output of a market, and every valuation technique later in this curriculum is a way of asking whether that price looks sensible. You cannot argue a stock is cheap without understanding what produced the price in the first place." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. A primary market is where a claim is created and sold for the first time — a company issuing new shares, a government issuing a new bond. Money reaches the issuer here.<br><br>2. A secondary market is where existing claims change hands between investors. The company gets nothing; ownership just moves.<br><br>3. Buyers post the price they will pay (the bid), sellers post the price they will accept (the ask), and trades happen where the two meet.<br><br>4. The gap between bid and ask, and how much can trade without moving the price, is what people mean by liquidity." },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>A company sells 1 crore new shares at ₹100 each in an initial public offering.</p><ol><li>Primary market: the company receives ₹100 crore (before fees). New money enters the business.</li><li>The next day, an investor sells 100 of those shares to someone else at ₹112.</li><li>Secondary market: ₹11,200 moves between two investors. The company receives nothing from this trade.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> Only the first transaction funded the business. Most trading you see reported daily is the second kind — ownership changing hands, not money reaching companies.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Primary market", h: "Where new claims are issued and money reaches the issuer." },
      { t: "def", term: "Secondary market", h: "Where existing claims are traded between investors." },
      { t: "def", term: "Bid / ask", h: "The highest price a buyer will pay and the lowest a seller will accept." },
      { t: "def", term: "Liquidity", h: "How easily you can buy or sell without moving the price much." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> A company raises ₹200 crore in an IPO. Next week ₹500 crore of its shares trade on the exchange. How much of that ₹500 crore reaches the company?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Nothing. The ₹200 crore primary issue funded the company; the ₹500 crore is secondary trading — ownership changing hands between investors.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> A share quotes bid ₹98 / ask ₹100. You buy at the ask and immediately sell at the bid. What did the round trip cost as a percentage?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">You paid ₹100 and received ₹98: a ₹2 loss on ₹100 = 2%. The bid-ask spread is the cost of immediacy.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> Why is 'the last traded price' a shaky estimate of what you would get for selling a very large holding?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">The last price reflects one recent, typically small trade. A large sale must find many buyers, and pushing through the available bids moves the price down — the liquidity problem.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Markets exist to trade promises about future money and to price them.<br>• Only primary-market issuance actually funds a company.<br>• A quoted price is the result of one recent trade, not a statement of what something is worth." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Believing a company receives money when its shares trade on an exchange.<br><br>Treating the last traded price as the price at which you could sell a large holding." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "In which market does a company actually receive money?", opts: ["Secondary market", "Primary market", "Both equally", "Neither"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Only primary issuance — new shares or bonds sold by the issuer — funds the company. Secondary trading moves ownership between investors.", "Correct. Only primary issuance — new shares or bonds sold by the issuer — funds the company. Secondary trading moves ownership between investors.", "Not this one. Only primary issuance — new shares or bonds sold by the issuer — funds the company. Secondary trading moves ownership between investors.", "Not this one. Only primary issuance — new shares or bonds sold by the issuer — funds the company. Secondary trading moves ownership between investors."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "The bid is…", opts: ["The lowest price a seller will accept", "The highest price a buyer will pay", "Yesterday's closing price", "The IPO price"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Buyers post bids; sellers post asks. Trades happen where they meet, and the gap between them is a real trading cost.", "Correct. Buyers post bids; sellers post asks. Trades happen where they meet, and the gap between them is a real trading cost.", "Not this one. Buyers post bids; sellers post asks. Trades happen where they meet, and the gap between them is a real trading cost.", "Not this one. Buyers post bids; sellers post asks. Trades happen where they meet, and the gap between them is a real trading cost."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "A liquid market is one where…", opts: ["Prices only rise", "You can trade size without moving the price much", "There are no sellers", "The government sets prices"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Liquidity is the ability to buy or sell near the prevailing price. Thin markets punish large orders.", "Correct. Liquidity is the ability to buy or sell near the prevailing price. Thin markets punish large orders.", "Not this one. Liquidity is the ability to buy or sell near the prevailing price. Thin markets punish large orders.", "Not this one. Liquidity is the ability to buy or sell near the prevailing price. Thin markets punish large orders."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-companies-and-capital"] = {
    id: "c-companies-and-capital", minutes: 5,
    title: "Companies and capital",
    short: "Companies and capital",
    desc: "A company needs money to operate, and it can only get it from two places: owners or lenders.",
    lede: "To run a business you need money before you earn any — for premises, equipment, stock and salaries.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "To run a business you need money before you earn any — for premises, equipment, stock and salaries. That money is called capital, and there are only two sources. Owners put money in and receive a share of whatever is left over (equity). Lenders put money in and are owed a fixed amount back (debt). Everything about a company's financing is a choice between those two." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "The split between owner money and borrowed money determines who bears risk, who gets paid first when things go wrong, and how much of the profit each side keeps. It is the single most consequential structural choice a company makes." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Owners contribute equity. They are paid last and have no guarantee, but they keep everything that remains after other claims.<br><br>2. Lenders contribute debt. They are paid before owners and their return is fixed by contract, so their upside is capped.<br><br>3. The company uses the combined pool to buy assets and run operations.<br><br>4. Profits first service the debt; whatever survives belongs to the owners." },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>Starting a bakery needs ₹50 lakh. You put in ₹30 lakh of your own money and borrow ₹20 lakh at 10%.</p><ol><li>Interest owed each year = ₹20,00,000 × 10% = ₹2,00,000.</li><li>In a good year the bakery earns ₹8,00,000 before interest. After ₹2,00,000 interest, you keep ₹6,00,000 on your ₹30 lakh — a 20% return.</li><li>In a bad year it earns ₹1,50,000. Interest of ₹2,00,000 is still owed in full, so you are ₹50,000 short and must fund the gap.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> Borrowing magnified both outcomes. The lender's ₹2,00,000 never changed; all the variability landed on you. That is what it means to be the residual owner.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Capital", h: "The money a business uses to operate and grow." },
      { t: "def", term: "Equity", h: "Owner-supplied capital, paid last, with unlimited upside and downside." },
      { t: "def", term: "Debt", h: "Borrowed capital, paid before owners, with a fixed contractual return." },
      { t: "def", term: "Residual claim", h: "The right to whatever is left after everyone else is paid — what owners hold." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> A bakery needs ₹50 lakh: ₹30 lakh from you, ₹20 lakh borrowed at 10%. It earns ₹8 lakh before interest. What do you keep, and what return is that on your money?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Interest = ₹2,00,000. You keep ₹6,00,000 on ₹30,00,000 invested = 20%.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> Same bakery earns only ₹1,50,000 before interest in a bad year. What happens?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Interest of ₹2,00,000 is still owed in full. You are ₹50,000 short and must fund the gap — the lender's claim does not flex with performance.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> Why is debt cheaper than equity for the same business?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Lenders are paid first and their return is fixed, so they bear less risk. Less risk borne means less compensation demanded.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "There are only two sources of capital: owners and lenders.<br>• Lenders get paid first and get a fixed amount; owners get paid last and get whatever is left.<br>• Borrowing amplifies good years and bad years alike for the owners." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Thinking debt is simply bad. It is cheaper than equity precisely because it is safer for the provider.<br><br>Forgetting that interest is owed whether or not the business had a good year." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "The residual claim on a business belongs to…", opts: ["Suppliers", "Lenders", "Owners", "Employees"], correct: 2, why: ["Not this one. Owners get whatever remains after every other claim — unlimited upside, first in line for losses.", "Not this one. Owners get whatever remains after every other claim — unlimited upside, first in line for losses.", "Correct. Owners get whatever remains after every other claim — unlimited upside, first in line for losses.", "Not this one. Owners get whatever remains after every other claim — unlimited upside, first in line for losses."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "A company earns ₹10 crore before interest and owes ₹3 crore of interest. In a year it earns only ₹2 crore, the interest owed is…", opts: ["₹0", "₹2 crore", "₹3 crore", "Whatever the owners decide"], correct: 2, why: ["Not this one. Interest is contractual. It is ₹3 crore whether the year was good or terrible — that fixity is the whole nature of debt.", "Not this one. Interest is contractual. It is ₹3 crore whether the year was good or terrible — that fixity is the whole nature of debt.", "Correct. Interest is contractual. It is ₹3 crore whether the year was good or terrible — that fixity is the whole nature of debt.", "Not this one. Interest is contractual. It is ₹3 crore whether the year was good or terrible — that fixity is the whole nature of debt."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "There are fundamentally how many sources of capital for a business?", opts: ["One", "Two — owners and lenders", "Four", "Unlimited"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Every instrument, however exotic, is a variation on owner money (equity) or borrowed money (debt).", "Correct. Every instrument, however exotic, is a variation on owner money (equity) or borrowed money (debt).", "Not this one. Every instrument, however exotic, is a variation on owner money (equity) or borrowed money (debt).", "Not this one. Every instrument, however exotic, is a variation on owner money (equity) or borrowed money (debt)."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-revenue"] = {
    id: "c-revenue", minutes: 5,
    title: "Revenue",
    short: "Revenue",
    desc: "The total value of what a business sold in a period — before any costs are taken out.",
    lede: "Revenue is the value of everything a business sold to customers during a period.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Revenue is the value of everything a business sold to customers during a period. It sits at the very top of the income statement, which is why people call it the top line. Two warnings from the start: revenue is not profit, because no costs have been subtracted yet, and revenue is not cash, because customers may not have paid yet." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Revenue is the starting point of almost every financial analysis and forecast. It is also the number most easily misread — a company can grow revenue while losing money and running out of cash at the same time." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. For a simple business, revenue is the number of units sold multiplied by the price per unit.<br><br>2. Revenue is recorded when the business delivers what it promised, not necessarily when the customer pays.<br><br>3. If the customer has not paid yet, the business records an amount owed to it, called a receivable.<br><br>4. Growth in revenue comes from selling more units, charging more per unit, selling a different mix of products, or buying another business." },
      { t: "formula", title: "Total sales value for a period, for a single-product business", lines: ["<b>Revenue = Price × Volume</b>", "Price = What one unit sells for", "Volume = How many units were sold in the period"] },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>A café sells 20,000 cups of coffee in a year at ₹150 per cup.</p><ol><li>Revenue = 20,000 × ₹150 = ₹30,00,000 (₹30 lakh).</li><li>Next year volume rises 10% to 22,000 cups and price rises 5% to ₹157.50.</li><li>New revenue = 22,000 × ₹157.50 = ₹34,65,000.</li><li>Growth = ₹34,65,000 ÷ ₹30,00,000 − 1 = 15.5%.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> Revenue grew 15.5%, not 15%. Price and volume growth multiply rather than add. Splitting growth into its price and volume parts tells you whether customers are buying more or simply paying more.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Top line", h: "Another name for revenue, from its position at the top of the income statement." },
      { t: "def", term: "Volume", h: "The number of units sold." },
      { t: "def", term: "Receivable", h: "Money a customer owes for goods already delivered." },
      { t: "def", term: "Organic growth", h: "Growth from the existing business, excluding acquisitions." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> A café sells 20,000 cups at ₹150. Next year volume rises 10% and price rises 5%. Calculate both years' revenue and the growth rate.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Year 1: 20,000 × ₹150 = ₹30,00,000. Year 2: 22,000 × ₹157.50 = ₹34,65,000. Growth = 15.5% — more than 10% + 5% because the effects multiply.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> A firm reports 18% revenue growth, of which 12 points came from an acquisition. What was organic growth, and why does the split matter?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Organic growth ≈ 6%. Acquired revenue was bought, not grown; it says little about whether existing customers are buying more.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> A software company invoices ₹40 lakh in March, collecting in May. What does March's income statement show, and what appears on the balance sheet?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">March revenue of ₹40 lakh, because delivery happened. A ₹40 lakh receivable appears — revenue recognised, cash not yet received.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Revenue is sales value before any costs — it says nothing about profitability.<br>• Revenue is recorded on delivery, not on payment, so it is not the same as cash received.<br>• Always ask whether growth came from price, volume, mix or acquisitions." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Treating revenue as money in the bank.<br><br>Calling revenue 'income'. In accounting, income usually means profit.<br><br>Presenting growth from an acquisition as if the existing business grew." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "A shop sells goods worth ₹5,00,000 in June; customers pay in July. June revenue is…", opts: ["₹0", "₹5,00,000", "₹2,50,000", "Whatever cash arrived"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Revenue follows delivery, not payment. June also gains a ₹5,00,000 receivable.", "Correct. Revenue follows delivery, not payment. June also gains a ₹5,00,000 receivable.", "Not this one. Revenue follows delivery, not payment. June also gains a ₹5,00,000 receivable.", "Not this one. Revenue follows delivery, not payment. June also gains a ₹5,00,000 receivable."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Price rises 10% and volume rises 10%. Revenue rises…", opts: ["Exactly 20%", "21%", "10%", "It cannot be determined"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. 1.10 × 1.10 = 1.21. Price and volume growth multiply rather than add.", "Correct. 1.10 × 1.10 = 1.21. Price and volume growth multiply rather than add.", "Not this one. 1.10 × 1.10 = 1.21. Price and volume growth multiply rather than add.", "Not this one. 1.10 × 1.10 = 1.21. Price and volume growth multiply rather than add."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Which is a warning sign about growth quality?", opts: ["Growth driven by volume", "Growth driven entirely by an acquisition presented as organic", "Growth with stable prices", "Growth in a new region"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Acquired revenue is purchased. Presenting it as organic growth misstates how the underlying business is performing.", "Correct. Acquired revenue is purchased. Presenting it as organic growth misstates how the underlying business is performing.", "Not this one. Acquired revenue is purchased. Presenting it as organic growth misstates how the underlying business is performing.", "Not this one. Acquired revenue is purchased. Presenting it as organic growth misstates how the underlying business is performing."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-costs"] = {
    id: "c-costs", minutes: 5,
    title: "Costs",
    short: "Costs",
    desc: "What a business consumes to produce and sell — and how those amounts respond when sales change.",
    lede: "Costs are the resources a business uses up in order to earn its revenue: ingredients, wages, rent, electricity, marketing.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Costs are the resources a business uses up in order to earn its revenue: ingredients, wages, rent, electricity, marketing. The useful question for a beginner is not what each cost is called, but how it behaves. Some costs rise automatically when you sell more. Others stay flat whatever happens." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Cost behaviour determines whether growth makes a business more profitable or just busier. Two companies with identical revenue and identical total costs can respond in completely opposite ways to a 20% sales increase." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Variable costs move with sales volume. Sell twice as many coffees and you buy roughly twice as many beans.<br><br>2. Fixed costs do not move with sales in the short run. Rent is the same whether you serve 50 customers or 500.<br><br>3. Some costs are neither: they hold flat and then jump, such as hiring a second shift when the first one is full.<br><br>4. Because fixed costs are spread over more units as sales grow, profit usually grows faster than revenue." },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>A café charges ₹150 a cup. Ingredients and cups cost ₹50 per cup. Rent, salaries and utilities total ₹15,00,000 a year.</p><ol><li>At 20,000 cups: revenue ₹30,00,000, variable costs 20,000 × ₹50 = ₹10,00,000, fixed costs ₹15,00,000. Profit = ₹5,00,000.</li><li>At 24,000 cups (20% more): revenue ₹36,00,000, variable costs ₹12,00,000, fixed costs still ₹15,00,000. Profit = ₹9,00,000.</li><li>Revenue rose 20%. Profit rose from ₹5,00,000 to ₹9,00,000, which is 80%.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> The extra 4,000 cups brought in ₹6,00,000 of revenue but only ₹2,00,000 of extra cost, because rent did not change. This amplification is called operating leverage — and it works just as violently in reverse when sales fall.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Variable cost", h: "A cost that rises and falls with the number of units sold." },
      { t: "def", term: "Fixed cost", h: "A cost that stays the same in the short run regardless of sales." },
      { t: "def", term: "Contribution", h: "Price minus variable cost per unit — what each extra sale contributes to covering fixed costs." },
      { t: "def", term: "Operating leverage", h: "The tendency of profit to move by a larger percentage than revenue, caused by fixed costs." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> A café: price ₹150/cup, variable cost ₹50/cup, fixed costs ₹15,00,000. Compute profit at 20,000 and at 24,000 cups, and compare the percentage changes in revenue and profit.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">At 20,000: (150−50)×20,000 − 15,00,000 = ₹5,00,000. At 24,000: ₹9,00,000. Revenue rose 20%; profit rose 80% — fixed costs amplified the move.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> Classify by behaviour: barista wages on hourly shifts, shop rent, milk, the manager's salary.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Milk: variable. Rent and manager's salary: fixed. Hourly shift wages: step-like — flat until an extra shift is added.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> Why is a high-fixed-cost airline more dangerous in a downturn than a high-variable-cost caterer with the same margins today?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">The airline's costs barely fall when demand falls, so losses appear quickly. The caterer sheds variable cost with volume, cushioning the fall — operating leverage cuts both ways.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Classify costs by how they behave, not by their name.<br>• Fixed costs make profits swing more than revenue in both directions.<br>• A business with high fixed costs is more profitable when busy and more fragile when quiet." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Assuming every cost scales neatly with revenue when forecasting.<br><br>Treating 'fixed' as permanent. Rent is fixed this year and negotiable at renewal." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "A cost that stays flat when sales change in the short run is…", opts: ["Variable", "Fixed", "Sunk", "Marginal"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Fixed costs — rent, salaried staff — do not move with volume in the short run, which is exactly what creates operating leverage.", "Correct. Fixed costs — rent, salaried staff — do not move with volume in the short run, which is exactly what creates operating leverage.", "Not this one. Fixed costs — rent, salaried staff — do not move with volume in the short run, which is exactly what creates operating leverage.", "Not this one. Fixed costs — rent, salaried staff — do not move with volume in the short run, which is exactly what creates operating leverage."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "With ₹100 contribution per unit and ₹15,00,000 fixed costs, profit at 20,000 units is ₹5,00,000. Sales rise 20%. Profit becomes…", opts: ["₹6,00,000", "₹9,00,000", "₹5,00,000", "₹10,00,000"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. 4,000 extra units × ₹100 contribution = ₹4,00,000 more profit, all of it flowing through because fixed costs did not move.", "Correct. 4,000 extra units × ₹100 contribution = ₹4,00,000 more profit, all of it flowing through because fixed costs did not move.", "Not this one. 4,000 extra units × ₹100 contribution = ₹4,00,000 more profit, all of it flowing through because fixed costs did not move.", "Not this one. 4,000 extra units × ₹100 contribution = ₹4,00,000 more profit, all of it flowing through because fixed costs did not move."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Operating leverage means…", opts: ["Borrowing to operate", "Profit swings by a larger percentage than revenue", "Costs equal revenue", "Fixed costs are zero"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Fixed costs make each incremental sale unusually profitable — and each lost sale unusually painful.", "Correct. Fixed costs make each incremental sale unusually profitable — and each lost sale unusually painful.", "Not this one. Fixed costs make each incremental sale unusually profitable — and each lost sale unusually painful.", "Not this one. Fixed costs make each incremental sale unusually profitable — and each lost sale unusually painful."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-profit"] = {
    id: "c-profit", minutes: 5,
    title: "Profit",
    short: "Profit",
    desc: "What is left from revenue after costs — an accounting measure of performance, not of cash.",
    lede: "Profit is what remains when you subtract costs from revenue.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Profit is what remains when you subtract costs from revenue. There is no single profit number: you get a different one depending on which costs you have subtracted so far. Subtract only the direct cost of what you sold and you get gross profit. Keep going through operating costs, then interest, then tax, and you eventually reach net profit — the amount that belongs to the owners." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Profit is the headline measure of whether a business works. But because it is calculated using accounting rules rather than bank statements, a company can report a healthy profit in a year when its cash balance fell. Knowing which profit is being quoted, and how it differs from cash, is a core skill." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Start with revenue.<br><br>2. Subtract the direct cost of producing what you sold to get gross profit.<br><br>3. Subtract the costs of running the business — salaries, rent, marketing — to get operating profit.<br><br>4. Subtract interest owed to lenders, then tax owed to the government, to get net profit." },
      { t: "formula", title: "The profit remaining for owners after every other claim", lines: ["<b>Net profit = Revenue − Costs − Interest − Tax</b>", "Revenue = Sales value for the period", "Costs = Everything consumed to produce and sell", "Interest = The amount owed to lenders for the period", "Tax = The amount owed to the government on the profit"] },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>A business has revenue of ₹100 crore, direct costs of ₹40 crore, operating costs of ₹35 crore, interest of ₹3 crore and a 25% tax rate.</p><ol><li>Gross profit = ₹100 crore − ₹40 crore = ₹60 crore.</li><li>Operating profit = ₹60 crore − ₹35 crore = ₹25 crore.</li><li>Profit before tax = ₹25 crore − ₹3 crore interest = ₹22 crore.</li><li>Tax = ₹22 crore × 25% = ₹5.5 crore.</li><li>Net profit = ₹22 crore − ₹5.5 crore = ₹16.5 crore.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> The same company can honestly be described as making ₹60 crore, ₹25 crore or ₹16.5 crore. Whenever someone quotes a profit figure, the first question is which line they mean.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Gross profit", h: "Revenue minus the direct cost of the goods or services sold." },
      { t: "def", term: "Operating profit", h: "Profit after all running costs but before interest and tax." },
      { t: "def", term: "Net profit", h: "The final profit belonging to owners, after interest and tax." },
      { t: "def", term: "Margin", h: "A profit figure expressed as a percentage of revenue." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> Revenue ₹100 crore, direct costs ₹40 crore, operating costs ₹35 crore, interest ₹3 crore, tax 25%. Build the bridge to net profit.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Gross ₹60 crore → operating ₹25 crore → PBT ₹22 crore → tax ₹5.5 crore → net ₹16.5 crore.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> The same company's net margin, in one calculation.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">₹16.5 crore ÷ ₹100 crore = 16.5%.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> A firm's net profit rose 30% thanks to a one-time sale of land. Why should you not conclude the business improved 30%?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">The gain is non-recurring. Strip it out and compare operating profit year over year — that is what the business itself earned.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "There are several profit figures; always establish which one is being quoted.<br>• Profit is calculated under accounting rules and is not the same as cash generated.<br>• Margins let you compare profitability across businesses of different sizes." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Assuming profit equals the increase in the bank balance.<br><br>Comparing one company's gross profit with another's net profit.<br><br>Judging a single year's profit without checking for one-off gains or losses." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Gross profit is revenue minus…", opts: ["All costs", "Direct costs of goods sold", "Interest and tax", "Operating expenses"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Gross profit isolates the economics of delivering the product, before running costs, interest and tax.", "Correct. Gross profit isolates the economics of delivering the product, before running costs, interest and tax.", "Not this one. Gross profit isolates the economics of delivering the product, before running costs, interest and tax.", "Not this one. Gross profit isolates the economics of delivering the product, before running costs, interest and tax."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Revenue ₹50 lakh, COGS ₹20 lakh, opex ₹18 lakh, interest ₹2 lakh, tax 25%. Net profit is…", opts: ["₹12,00,000", "₹10,00,000", "₹7,50,000", "₹9,00,000"], correct: 2, why: ["Not this one. Gross ₹30L → operating ₹12L → PBT ₹10L → tax ₹2.5L → net ₹7.5L.", "Not this one. Gross ₹30L → operating ₹12L → PBT ₹10L → tax ₹2.5L → net ₹7.5L.", "Correct. Gross ₹30L → operating ₹12L → PBT ₹10L → tax ₹2.5L → net ₹7.5L.", "Not this one. Gross ₹30L → operating ₹12L → PBT ₹10L → tax ₹2.5L → net ₹7.5L."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "A company reports strong profit but its cash balance fell. The most likely explanation is…", opts: ["The accounts are fraudulent", "Timing: profit is accrual-based, cash is not", "Profit and cash are the same, so this is impossible", "Taxes were negative"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Receivables, inventory and capex absorb cash without reducing profit in the same period. Divergence is normal, not automatically sinister.", "Correct. Receivables, inventory and capex absorb cash without reducing profit in the same period. Divergence is normal, not automatically sinister.", "Not this one. Receivables, inventory and capex absorb cash without reducing profit in the same period. Divergence is normal, not automatically sinister.", "Not this one. Receivables, inventory and capex absorb cash without reducing profit in the same period. Divergence is normal, not automatically sinister."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-cash"] = {
    id: "c-cash", minutes: 5,
    title: "Cash",
    short: "Cash",
    desc: "Money actually available to spend today — the thing a business cannot survive without.",
    lede: "Cash is money you can spend right now: notes, and balances in bank accounts you can draw on immediately.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Cash is money you can spend right now: notes, and balances in bank accounts you can draw on immediately. It is deliberately narrow. A customer who owes you ₹10 lakh next month is not cash. Stock sitting in a warehouse is not cash. Only money you could pay a supplier with today counts." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Profit is an opinion formed under accounting rules; cash is a fact you can verify at the bank. Businesses do not fail because they are unprofitable — they fail because on some particular day they cannot pay someone. Profitable companies go under regularly for exactly this reason." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Cash rises when customers pay you, when you borrow, and when owners invest.<br><br>2. Cash falls when you pay suppliers and staff, buy equipment, repay loans and pay tax.<br><br>3. Cash timing rarely matches profit timing: you often pay for stock long before a customer pays you.<br><br>4. The gap between profit and cash is mostly explained by that timing, plus spending on long-lived assets." },
      { t: "formula", title: "How the cash balance changed over a period", lines: ["<b>Ending cash = Starting cash + Cash in − Cash out</b>", "Starting cash = The balance at the beginning of the period", "Cash in = Collections from customers, borrowing, money from owners", "Cash out = Payments to suppliers, staff, lenders, tax and for equipment"] },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>A shop reports a ₹10 lakh profit for the year, but the owner says the bank balance barely moved.</p><ol><li>Profit for the year: ₹10,00,000.</li><li>Customers owed ₹6,00,000 more at year-end than at the start — that revenue was earned but not collected.</li><li>Stock on the shelves rose by ₹3,00,000 — cash was spent on goods not yet sold.</li><li>Cash effect ≈ ₹10,00,000 − ₹6,00,000 − ₹3,00,000 = ₹1,00,000.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> The ₹10 lakh profit was real, but ₹9 lakh of it is sitting in customer invoices and warehouse shelves rather than the bank. This is the single most common way a growing business runs out of money.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Cash equivalent", h: "A very short-term, very safe holding that can be turned into cash almost immediately." },
      { t: "def", term: "Liquidity", h: "How easily something can be turned into spendable cash." },
      { t: "def", term: "Restricted cash", h: "Cash the business holds but is not free to spend." },
      { t: "def", term: "Insolvency", h: "Being unable to pay obligations when they fall due." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> Profit ₹10,00,000; receivables rose ₹6,00,000; inventory rose ₹3,00,000. Estimate the cash generated.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">₹10,00,000 − ₹6,00,000 − ₹3,00,000 = ₹1,00,000. The other ₹9 lakh sits in invoices and stock.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> Which of these can pay next week's salaries: ₹20 lakh bank balance, ₹30 lakh of receivables due in 60 days, ₹25 lakh of inventory?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Only the ₹20 lakh in the bank. The rest becomes cash later — if customers pay and stock sells.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> Explain in two sentences how a profitable, growing business runs out of money.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Growth means buying more stock and carrying more unpaid invoices, both of which consume cash before the profit is collected. If funding does not keep pace, the business cannot pay someone on some particular day — which is failure.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Cash is spendable today; almost nothing else on the balance sheet is.<br>• Profit and cash differ mainly because of timing and spending on long-lived assets.<br>• Growing businesses consume cash even when profitable, because stock and unpaid invoices grow first." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Assuming a large cash balance means the business is safe, without checking what it owes next month.<br><br>Counting money owed by customers as cash." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Which counts as cash?", opts: ["A receivable due next month", "Stock in the warehouse", "A bank balance available today", "The owner's car"], correct: 2, why: ["Not this one. Cash is deliberately narrow: money spendable today. Everything else must first be converted.", "Not this one. Cash is deliberately narrow: money spendable today. Everything else must first be converted.", "Correct. Cash is deliberately narrow: money spendable today. Everything else must first be converted.", "Not this one. Cash is deliberately narrow: money spendable today. Everything else must first be converted."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Businesses fail when…", opts: ["Profit margins dip", "They cannot pay an obligation on the day it falls due", "Revenue growth slows", "Depreciation rises"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Insolvency is a cash event. Profitable companies fail this way regularly.", "Correct. Insolvency is a cash event. Profitable companies fail this way regularly.", "Not this one. Insolvency is a cash event. Profitable companies fail this way regularly.", "Not this one. Insolvency is a cash event. Profitable companies fail this way regularly."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Start cash ₹20 crore; operations bring ₹8 crore; capex ₹5 crore; new debt ₹3 crore. Ending cash is…", opts: ["₹26 crore", "₹20 crore", "₹31 crore", "₹16 crore"], correct: 0, why: ["Correct. 20 + 8 − 5 + 3 = ₹26 crore. Ending cash = start + inflows − outflows, across operations, investment and financing.", "Not this one. 20 + 8 − 5 + 3 = ₹26 crore. Ending cash = start + inflows − outflows, across operations, investment and financing.", "Not this one. 20 + 8 − 5 + 3 = ₹26 crore. Ending cash = start + inflows − outflows, across operations, investment and financing.", "Not this one. 20 + 8 − 5 + 3 = ₹26 crore. Ending cash = start + inflows − outflows, across operations, investment and financing."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-assets"] = {
    id: "c-assets", minutes: 5,
    title: "Assets",
    short: "Assets",
    desc: "The things a business controls that it expects to produce value in future.",
    lede: "An asset is something the business controls and expects to get future benefit from.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "An asset is something the business controls and expects to get future benefit from. Cash, money owed by customers, stock, machinery, buildings and software licences are all assets. They differ enormously in how quickly they can be turned into cash — which matters more than their size." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "The asset side of a balance sheet tells you where a company's capital has gone. Two businesses with ₹100 crore of assets are in completely different positions if one holds cash and the other holds unsold inventory." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Assets are usually listed in order of how quickly they become cash.<br><br>2. Current assets are expected to convert to cash within about a year: cash itself, receivables, inventory.<br><br>3. Non-current assets are held for longer: buildings, machinery, vehicles, long-term intangibles.<br><br>4. Assets are generally recorded at what the business paid for them, which may be nothing like what they are worth today." },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>Two businesses each report ₹100 crore of total assets.</p><ol><li>Business A: ₹60 crore cash, ₹20 crore receivables, ₹20 crore equipment.</li><li>Business B: ₹5 crore cash, ₹15 crore receivables, ₹80 crore of specialised machinery.</li><li>Both must pay a ₹25 crore bill next month.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> A pays it easily. B cannot — you cannot settle an invoice with machinery, and selling specialised equipment quickly usually means selling it cheaply. Identical asset totals, very different situations.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Current asset", h: "An asset expected to become cash within roughly a year." },
      { t: "def", term: "Non-current asset", h: "An asset held for longer than a year, such as property or equipment." },
      { t: "def", term: "Book value", h: "The amount an asset is carried at in the accounts, usually based on original cost." },
      { t: "def", term: "Intangible asset", h: "A non-physical asset such as a licence, patent or brand." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> Sort by speed of becoming cash: specialised machinery, cash, 45-day receivables, seasonal fashion inventory.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Cash → receivables (≈45 days) → seasonal inventory (must sell before season ends, discount risk) → specialised machinery (few buyers, slow, price-cut risk).</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> Two firms each show ₹100 crore of assets. A: ₹60 crore cash + ₹40 crore equipment. B: ₹5 crore cash + ₹95 crore custom machinery. A ₹25 crore bill is due next month. Compare their positions.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">A pays comfortably from cash. B cannot — machinery cannot settle invoices and cannot be sold quickly at book value. Same total, opposite situations.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> Why might a building bought in 1995 for ₹2 crore still appear near that figure while being worth ₹20 crore?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Assets are generally carried at historical cost (less depreciation), not market value. Book value records what was paid, not what it would fetch.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Assets are resources expected to generate future benefit.<br>• How fast an asset becomes cash matters more than how large it is.<br>• Book value reflects past cost, not current market value." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Assuming an asset could be sold for the value shown in the accounts.<br><br>Treating all assets as equally available to meet a bill." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "An asset is…", opts: ["Anything expensive", "A resource controlled by the business with expected future benefit", "Only physical property", "Money owed to suppliers"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Control plus expected future benefit is the test — which is why receivables and licences qualify and decoration does not.", "Correct. Control plus expected future benefit is the test — which is why receivables and licences qualify and decoration does not.", "Not this one. Control plus expected future benefit is the test — which is why receivables and licences qualify and decoration does not.", "Not this one. Control plus expected future benefit is the test — which is why receivables and licences qualify and decoration does not."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "A current asset is one expected to become cash within roughly…", opts: ["A week", "A year", "Five years", "Its useful life"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. The one-year line separates current assets (cash, receivables, inventory) from non-current ones (property, equipment).", "Correct. The one-year line separates current assets (cash, receivables, inventory) from non-current ones (property, equipment).", "Not this one. The one-year line separates current assets (cash, receivables, inventory) from non-current ones (property, equipment).", "Not this one. The one-year line separates current assets (cash, receivables, inventory) from non-current ones (property, equipment)."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Book value of an asset generally reflects…", opts: ["Today's market price", "Original cost, adjusted by rules like depreciation", "The insured value", "What a buyer offered last week"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Accounts record cost-based values. Market worth can be far higher or lower — a distinction analysts must hold onto.", "Correct. Accounts record cost-based values. Market worth can be far higher or lower — a distinction analysts must hold onto.", "Not this one. Accounts record cost-based values. Market worth can be far higher or lower — a distinction analysts must hold onto.", "Not this one. Accounts record cost-based values. Market worth can be far higher or lower — a distinction analysts must hold onto."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-liabilities"] = {
    id: "c-liabilities", minutes: 5,
    title: "Liabilities",
    short: "Liabilities",
    desc: "What the business owes to people other than its owners.",
    lede: "A liability is an obligation the business already has because of something that has already happened.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "A liability is an obligation the business already has because of something that has already happened. It owes a supplier for goods delivered, a bank for money borrowed, staff for work performed, the government for tax on profits earned. Debt is one kind of liability, but far from the only kind." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Liabilities are claims that rank ahead of the owners. When you assess risk, the questions are always the same: how much is owed, to whom, and — crucially — when." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Current liabilities are due within about a year: supplier invoices, salaries payable, short-term borrowing.<br><br>2. Non-current liabilities are due later: long-term loans, bonds.<br><br>3. Some liabilities carry interest (loans) and some do not (a supplier invoice due in 30 days).<br><br>4. Every liability has a date attached, and that timing determines whether it is a problem." },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>A business owes ₹70 crore in total.</p><ol><li>Case 1: ₹10 crore of supplier invoices due next month, ₹60 crore of loans repayable in eight years.</li><li>Case 2: ₹10 crore of supplier invoices due next month, ₹60 crore of loans repayable in four months.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> The total is identical. Case 1 is manageable; Case 2 is a business that needs to refinance ₹60 crore within four months or fail. Reading only the total would have told you nothing.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Current liability", h: "An obligation due within roughly a year." },
      { t: "def", term: "Payable", h: "Money owed to a supplier for goods or services already received." },
      { t: "def", term: "Accrued expense", h: "A cost already incurred but not yet invoiced or paid." },
      { t: "def", term: "Maturity", h: "The date a liability must be repaid." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> A firm owes ₹70 crore: ₹10 crore supplier invoices due next month and ₹60 crore of loans. Compare the risk if the loans mature in 8 years versus 4 months.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Identical totals. With 8-year maturity, next month's need is ₹10 crore — manageable. With 4-month maturity, ₹60 crore must be repaid or refinanced almost immediately — a survival question.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> Which of these liabilities charge interest: bank loan, supplier invoice on 30-day terms, salaries payable, bonds issued?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">The bank loan and bonds. Standard supplier credit and accrued salaries are obligations but typically interest-free.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> Total liabilities ₹90 crore; debt ₹40 crore. What is the other ₹50 crore likely made of?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Non-debt obligations: supplier payables, accrued expenses, tax payable, deferred revenue — claims that arose from operating, not borrowing.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "A liability is a present obligation arising from a past event.<br>• Not all liabilities are debt, and not all liabilities charge interest.<br>• Timing matters as much as size — always look at when amounts fall due." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Equating total liabilities with debt.<br><br>Comparing two companies on total liabilities without checking maturity dates." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "A liability is…", opts: ["Any future plan to spend", "A present obligation from a past event", "Only bank borrowing", "The owners' stake"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. The event has already happened — goods received, money borrowed, work performed — so the obligation already exists.", "Correct. The event has already happened — goods received, money borrowed, work performed — so the obligation already exists.", "Not this one. The event has already happened — goods received, money borrowed, work performed — so the obligation already exists.", "Not this one. The event has already happened — goods received, money borrowed, work performed — so the obligation already exists."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Debt and liabilities relate how?", opts: ["They are identical", "Debt is one kind of liability", "Liabilities are one kind of debt", "Neither involves obligations"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. All debt is a liability, but payables, accruals and deferred revenue are liabilities that are not debt.", "Correct. All debt is a liability, but payables, accruals and deferred revenue are liabilities that are not debt.", "Not this one. All debt is a liability, but payables, accruals and deferred revenue are liabilities that are not debt.", "Not this one. All debt is a liability, but payables, accruals and deferred revenue are liabilities that are not debt."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Two companies owe the same total. What most changes the risk picture?", opts: ["The font of the annual report", "When the amounts fall due", "The number of creditors", "The company's age"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Timing decides whether an obligation is routine or existential. Always read the maturity schedule.", "Correct. Timing decides whether an obligation is routine or existential. Always read the maturity schedule.", "Not this one. Timing decides whether an obligation is routine or existential. Always read the maturity schedule.", "Not this one. Timing decides whether an obligation is routine or existential. Always read the maturity schedule."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-equity"] = {
    id: "c-equity", minutes: 5,
    title: "Equity",
    short: "Equity",
    desc: "What the owners are left with after everything owed to others is settled.",
    lede: "Equity is the owners' share of a business: everything the business has, minus everything it owes.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Equity is the owners' share of a business: everything the business has, minus everything it owes. It is a residual — you find it by subtraction, not by looking it up. It comes from two sources: money the owners put in, and profits the business earned and kept rather than paying out." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Equity is the buffer that absorbs losses before lenders are affected. It is also the base against which owner returns are measured. And its accounting value is usually very different from what the business would sell for, which is a distinction beginners must get right early." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Owners contribute money, which is recorded as paid-in capital.<br><br>2. The business earns profits. Any profit not paid out to owners is retained and added to equity.<br><br>3. Losses reduce equity. Payments to owners (dividends) reduce it too.<br><br>4. Equity always equals assets minus liabilities — that relationship holds by construction." },
      { t: "formula", title: "The owners' residual claim on the business", lines: ["<b>Equity = Assets − Liabilities</b>", "Assets = Everything the business controls", "Liabilities = Everything the business owes to non-owners"] },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>A business has ₹150 crore of assets and ₹90 crore of liabilities.</p><ol><li>Equity = ₹150 crore − ₹90 crore = ₹60 crore.</li><li>It then makes a ₹12 crore profit and pays ₹5 crore to owners as a dividend.</li><li>Equity = ₹60 crore + ₹12 crore − ₹5 crore = ₹67 crore.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> The ₹67 crore is the accounting value of the owners' stake. If the business were listed, investors might value it at ₹200 crore or ₹30 crore. Accounting equity records what was put in and kept; market value reflects what people expect it to earn.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Paid-in capital", h: "Money owners contributed directly to the business." },
      { t: "def", term: "Retained earnings", h: "Accumulated profits kept in the business rather than paid out." },
      { t: "def", term: "Dividend", h: "A payment of profit from the business to its owners." },
      { t: "def", term: "Book equity", h: "The accounting value of the owners' stake, as distinct from market value." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> Assets ₹150 crore, liabilities ₹90 crore. Then the firm earns ₹12 crore and pays a ₹5 crore dividend. Equity before and after?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Before: ₹60 crore. After: 60 + 12 − 5 = ₹67 crore.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> A company's book equity is ₹67 crore but the market values its shares at ₹200 crore. Are the accounts wrong?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">No. Book equity records capital put in and profits kept; market value prices expected future earnings. They answer different questions.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> A firm makes a ₹15 crore loss. Who absorbs it first, and what does that mean for lenders?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Equity absorbs it — the owners' claim falls by ₹15 crore. Lenders are untouched unless losses exhaust the equity buffer, which is why more equity means safer debt.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Equity is a residual: assets minus liabilities.<br>• It grows through retained profit and shrinks through losses and dividends.<br>• Book equity and market value answer different questions and are rarely equal." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Confusing book equity with what the company is worth.<br><br>Forgetting that equity absorbs losses first, before lenders are touched." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Equity equals…", opts: ["Assets + liabilities", "Assets − liabilities", "Revenue − costs", "Cash in the bank"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Equity is the residual: whatever remains for owners after all other claims.", "Correct. Equity is the residual: whatever remains for owners after all other claims.", "Not this one. Equity is the residual: whatever remains for owners after all other claims.", "Not this one. Equity is the residual: whatever remains for owners after all other claims."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Equity grows when the business…", opts: ["Borrows more", "Retains profit", "Pays a dividend", "Buys equipment with cash"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Retained profit adds to equity. Borrowing raises liabilities; dividends reduce equity; swapping cash for equipment changes neither.", "Correct. Retained profit adds to equity. Borrowing raises liabilities; dividends reduce equity; swapping cash for equipment changes neither.", "Not this one. Retained profit adds to equity. Borrowing raises liabilities; dividends reduce equity; swapping cash for equipment changes neither.", "Not this one. Retained profit adds to equity. Borrowing raises liabilities; dividends reduce equity; swapping cash for equipment changes neither."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Book equity and market value of equity are…", opts: ["Always equal", "Different measures answering different questions", "Both set by the stock exchange", "Both equal to liquidation value"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Book equity is accounting history; market value is priced expectation. Confusing them is a classic beginner error.", "Correct. Book equity is accounting history; market value is priced expectation. Confusing them is a classic beginner error.", "Not this one. Book equity is accounting history; market value is priced expectation. Confusing them is a classic beginner error.", "Not this one. Book equity is accounting history; market value is priced expectation. Confusing them is a classic beginner error."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-debt"] = {
    id: "c-debt", minutes: 5,
    title: "Debt",
    short: "Debt",
    desc: "Borrowed money that must be repaid on agreed terms, whatever the business earns.",
    lede: "Debt is money borrowed under a contract: the business receives cash now and promises to pay interest and return the principal on specified dates.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Debt is money borrowed under a contract: the business receives cash now and promises to pay interest and return the principal on specified dates. The promise does not depend on how the business performs. That fixed, unconditional quality is what makes debt cheaper than equity for the borrower and safer for the provider." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Debt is the main lever a business has over its own risk. Used moderately it raises owner returns; used heavily it can destroy a business that would otherwise have survived a bad year." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. The lender advances the principal.<br><br>2. The borrower pays interest at agreed intervals — a cost that appears whether or not there is profit.<br><br>3. The principal is repaid at maturity, or in instalments along the way.<br><br>4. Because interest is contractual, cash flow that is more than enough in good years can be badly insufficient in weak ones." },
      { t: "formula", title: "Debt net of the cash available to repay it", lines: ["<b>Net debt = Total debt − Cash</b>", "Total debt = All borrowings outstanding", "Cash = Cash and cash equivalents on hand"] },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>Two identical businesses each earn ₹10 crore before interest. One has no debt; the other has ₹50 crore of debt at 10% and ₹50 crore of equity.</p><ol><li>No-debt business: earns ₹10 crore on ₹100 crore of equity — a 10% owner return.</li><li>Levered business: interest = ₹50 crore × 10% = ₹5 crore. Owners keep ₹5 crore on ₹50 crore of equity — also 10%.</li><li>Now earnings fall to ₹4 crore. No-debt business: 4% return. Levered business: ₹4 crore − ₹5 crore = a ₹1 crore loss.</li><li>Now earnings rise to ₹16 crore. No-debt: 16%. Levered: (₹16 − ₹5) ÷ ₹50 = 22%.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> Debt did not create value on its own. It stretched the range of outcomes for the owners in both directions. That stretch is the entire economics of borrowing.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Principal", h: "The amount borrowed, which must be repaid." },
      { t: "def", term: "Maturity", h: "The date by which the principal must be repaid." },
      { t: "def", term: "Net debt", h: "Total borrowings less cash on hand." },
      { t: "def", term: "Covenant", h: "A condition in a loan agreement the borrower must keep meeting." },
      { t: "def", term: "Default", h: "Failing to meet an obligation under the loan agreement." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> Two firms each earn ₹10 crore before interest. One is all-equity (₹100 crore). The other has ₹50 crore debt at 10% and ₹50 crore equity. Compute owner returns for both.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">All-equity: 10%. Levered: interest ₹5 crore, owners keep ₹5 crore on ₹50 crore = 10%. Identical here — the divergence appears when earnings move.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> Repeat with earnings of ₹16 crore, then ₹4 crore.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">At ₹16 crore: 16% vs (16−5)/50 = 22%. At ₹4 crore: 4% vs (4−5)/50 = −2%. Debt stretched the outcomes in both directions.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> Debt ₹100 crore, cash ₹25 crore. Net debt, and why the netting is meaningful?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">₹75 crore. Cash on hand could repay debt tomorrow, so net debt better reflects the true borrowing burden.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Debt obligations are fixed and do not flex with business performance.<br>• Borrowing widens the range of owner outcomes without itself creating value.<br>• Judge debt against the cash flow available to service it, not against profit alone." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Judging debt by its size rather than by the ability to service it.<br><br>Ignoring when the principal falls due.<br><br>Assuming low interest rates make any level of borrowing safe." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "What makes debt different from equity funding?", opts: ["It is always larger", "Its payments are contractual regardless of performance", "It carries voting rights", "It never has to be repaid"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Interest and principal are owed on schedule whatever the year looked like. That fixity is debt's defining feature.", "Correct. Interest and principal are owed on schedule whatever the year looked like. That fixity is debt's defining feature.", "Not this one. Interest and principal are owed on schedule whatever the year looked like. That fixity is debt's defining feature.", "Not this one. Interest and principal are owed on schedule whatever the year looked like. That fixity is debt's defining feature."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Borrowing at 10% to earn 10% on the business leaves owner returns…", opts: ["Higher", "Lower", "Unchanged", "Negative"], correct: 2, why: ["Not this one. Leverage only lifts returns when the business earns more than the borrowing cost. At equal rates it just adds risk for nothing.", "Not this one. Leverage only lifts returns when the business earns more than the borrowing cost. At equal rates it just adds risk for nothing.", "Correct. Leverage only lifts returns when the business earns more than the borrowing cost. At equal rates it just adds risk for nothing.", "Not this one. Leverage only lifts returns when the business earns more than the borrowing cost. At equal rates it just adds risk for nothing."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "The best single test of whether debt is safe is…", opts: ["Its absolute size", "The cash flow available to service it, and when it matures", "The lender's brand", "The interest rate alone"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. ₹500 crore of debt can be trivial or fatal — it depends on the cash generated against it and the repayment dates.", "Correct. ₹500 crore of debt can be trivial or fatal — it depends on the cash generated against it and the repayment dates.", "Not this one. ₹500 crore of debt can be trivial or fatal — it depends on the cash generated against it and the repayment dates.", "Not this one. ₹500 crore of debt can be trivial or fatal — it depends on the cash generated against it and the repayment dates."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-interest"] = {
    id: "c-interest", minutes: 5,
    title: "Interest",
    short: "Interest",
    desc: "The price paid for using someone else's money for a period of time.",
    lede: "Interest is rent on money.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Interest is rent on money. If you use ₹100 of someone else's money for a year, you pay them for that year of use. The rate is quoted as a percentage per period — almost always per year unless stated otherwise. Two rates that look identical can cost very different amounts if the periods or compounding differ." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Interest is the mechanism through which time has a price. Every discount rate, loan payment and bond yield in this curriculum is built on it." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Simple interest is charged only on the original principal.<br><br>2. Compound interest is charged on the principal plus interest already added, so the balance grows on itself.<br><br>3. How often interest is added matters: monthly compounding costs more over a year than annual compounding at the same quoted rate.<br><br>4. Always match the rate to the period — a 12% annual rate is 1% per month, not 12% per month." },
      { t: "formula", title: "Interest charged on the original principal only", lines: ["<b>Simple interest = Principal × Rate × Time</b>", "Principal = The amount borrowed or lent", "Rate = The interest rate per period, as a decimal", "Time = The number of periods, in the same units as the rate"] },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>You borrow ₹10,00,000 at 8% per year for 3 years.</p><ol><li>Simple interest: ₹10,00,000 × 8% × 3 = ₹2,40,000. Total repaid: ₹12,40,000.</li><li>Compound interest, added annually: Year 1 interest = ₹80,000, balance ₹10,80,000.</li><li>Year 2 interest = ₹10,80,000 × 8% = ₹86,400, balance ₹11,66,400.</li><li>Year 3 interest = ₹11,66,400 × 8% = ₹93,312, balance ₹12,59,712.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> Same rate, same term, ₹19,712 difference. Compounding charged you interest on interest. Over longer periods this gap becomes enormous, which is the subject of the next module.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Principal", h: "The original amount borrowed or invested." },
      { t: "def", term: "Simple interest", h: "Interest calculated on the principal only." },
      { t: "def", term: "Compound interest", h: "Interest calculated on principal plus previously added interest." },
      { t: "def", term: "Nominal rate", h: "The quoted annual rate, before accounting for compounding frequency." },
      { t: "def", term: "Effective rate", h: "The true annual cost once compounding frequency is included." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> ₹10,00,000 borrowed at 8% for 3 years. Total repaid under simple interest?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Interest = 10,00,000 × 8% × 3 = ₹2,40,000; total ₹12,40,000.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> Same loan with annual compounding — work the balance year by year.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Y1: ₹10,80,000. Y2: ₹11,66,400. Y3: ₹12,59,712. Compounding cost ₹19,712 more than simple.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> One lender quotes 12% annually; another quotes 1% monthly. Are they the same price?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">No. 1% monthly compounds to (1.01)¹² − 1 = 12.68% effective per year — the monthly quote is dearer.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Interest is the price of using money over time.<br>• Compounding charges interest on interest and always costs more than simple interest over multiple periods.<br>• A quoted rate is meaningless without knowing its period and compounding frequency." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Comparing a monthly rate with an annual rate directly.<br><br>Ignoring compounding frequency when comparing two loan offers." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Interest is best described as…", opts: ["A penalty for saving", "Rent paid for using money over time", "A tax", "Profit sharing"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. The borrower pays for the use of the lender's money for a period — rent on money.", "Correct. The borrower pays for the use of the lender's money for a period — rent on money.", "Not this one. The borrower pays for the use of the lender's money for a period — rent on money.", "Not this one. The borrower pays for the use of the lender's money for a period — rent on money."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "₹5,00,000 at 10% simple interest for 2 years accrues…", opts: ["₹1,00,000", "₹1,05,000", "₹50,000", "₹1,10,000"], correct: 0, why: ["Correct. Simple interest: 5,00,000 × 10% × 2 = ₹1,00,000. Compounding would add ₹5,000 more.", "Not this one. Simple interest: 5,00,000 × 10% × 2 = ₹1,00,000. Compounding would add ₹5,000 more.", "Not this one. Simple interest: 5,00,000 × 10% × 2 = ₹1,00,000. Compounding would add ₹5,000 more.", "Not this one. Simple interest: 5,00,000 × 10% × 2 = ₹1,00,000. Compounding would add ₹5,000 more."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Two loans quote 12% a year, one compounding annually and one monthly. Which costs more?", opts: ["Annual", "Monthly", "Identical", "Depends on the amount"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Monthly compounding charges interest on interest twelve times a year: effective 12.68% vs 12%.", "Correct. Monthly compounding charges interest on interest twelve times a year: effective 12.68% vs 12%.", "Not this one. Monthly compounding charges interest on interest twelve times a year: effective 12.68% vs 12%.", "Not this one. Monthly compounding charges interest on interest twelve times a year: effective 12.68% vs 12%."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-risk"] = {
    id: "c-risk", minutes: 5,
    title: "Risk",
    short: "Risk",
    desc: "The possibility that the outcome differs from what you expected — including permanently losing money.",
    lede: "Risk is the fact that you do not know what will happen.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Risk is the fact that you do not know what will happen. In finance it has two distinct meanings that beginners often merge. The first is volatility: how much a value bounces around. The second is permanent loss: the chance the money simply does not come back. A price that swings and recovers is volatile. A company that goes bankrupt is a permanent loss. They are not the same thing." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Risk is what you are paid to bear. Every extra percentage point of expected return in finance exists because someone accepted a risk to earn it. If you cannot name the risk you are taking, you cannot judge whether the return compensates you for it." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Identify what could differ from expectations, and by how much.<br><br>2. Separate risks that resolve over time from risks that destroy value permanently.<br><br>3. Ask whether the risk can be reduced by spreading money across many holdings, or whether it affects everything at once.<br><br>4. Compare the compensation offered against the risk actually taken." },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>Two ways to invest ₹1,00,000 for a year.</p><ol><li>Option A: a government bond paying 7%. You expect ₹1,07,000 with high confidence.</li><li>Option B: a share that might return 30%, might return −20%, and averages around 12%.</li><li>The extra expected 5% in Option B is the payment for accepting the −20% possibility.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> Option B is not better because its expected return is higher. It is a different trade: more expected return in exchange for the real chance of ending the year with ₹80,000. Whether that trade suits you depends on when you need the money.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Volatility", h: "How much a value fluctuates over time." },
      { t: "def", term: "Permanent loss of capital", h: "Money that is gone and will not recover." },
      { t: "def", term: "Diversification", h: "Spreading money across holdings so no single failure is decisive." },
      { t: "def", term: "Systematic risk", h: "Risk affecting the whole market, which diversification cannot remove." },
      { t: "def", term: "Credit risk", h: "The risk a borrower fails to pay what was promised." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> Stock A swung between ₹80 and ₹130 last year and sits at ₹120. Stock B moved calmly from ₹100 to ₹40 as the company failed. Which showed more volatility, and which delivered permanent loss?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">A was more volatile but recovered; B was calm on the way to destroying capital. Volatility and permanent loss are different risks.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> You own shares in one pharma company awaiting one drug approval. Which risk can diversification remove here, and which can it not?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Spreading across many companies removes the single-approval risk. It cannot remove market-wide risk — a crash that hits everything at once.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> A scheme offers 24% 'guaranteed, risk-free' when government bonds pay 7%. What is the correct inference?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">A 17-point premium exists only as payment for risk. If no risk is visible, it is hidden — often as credit or fraud risk. The claim itself is the warning.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Volatility and permanent loss are different risks and deserve different responses.<br>• Extra expected return always exists because a risk is being borne.<br>• Diversification reduces risks specific to one holding, not risks affecting everything." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Treating price fluctuation as the only form of risk.<br><br>Assuming that because something has not gone wrong yet, it cannot.<br><br>Believing a high expected return is free money rather than compensation." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Volatility and permanent loss of capital are…", opts: ["The same thing", "Different risks deserving different responses", "Both impossible to measure", "Only relevant to bonds"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. A price that swings and recovers has cost you nothing if you could wait. Money that does not come back is a different event entirely.", "Correct. A price that swings and recovers has cost you nothing if you could wait. Money that does not come back is a different event entirely.", "Not this one. A price that swings and recovers has cost you nothing if you could wait. Money that does not come back is a different event entirely.", "Not this one. A price that swings and recovers has cost you nothing if you could wait. Money that does not come back is a different event entirely."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Diversification protects mainly against…", opts: ["Market-wide crashes", "Risks specific to one holding", "Inflation", "All risk"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Many holdings mean no single failure is decisive. Systematic, market-wide risk remains.", "Correct. Many holdings mean no single failure is decisive. Systematic, market-wide risk remains.", "Not this one. Many holdings mean no single failure is decisive. Systematic, market-wide risk remains.", "Not this one. Many holdings mean no single failure is decisive. Systematic, market-wide risk remains."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Higher expected returns exist because…", opts: ["Markets are generous", "Someone is bearing more risk", "Of compounding", "Regulators require them"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Return is compensation. If you cannot name the risk being paid for, you have not found it yet.", "Correct. Return is compensation. If you cannot name the risk being paid for, you have not found it yet.", "Not this one. Return is compensation. If you cannot name the risk being paid for, you have not found it yet.", "Not this one. Return is compensation. If you cannot name the risk being paid for, you have not found it yet."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-return"] = {
    id: "c-return", minutes: 5,
    title: "Return",
    short: "Return",
    desc: "How much you gained or lost, measured against what you put in.",
    lede: "Return measures the result of an investment relative to its size.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Return measures the result of an investment relative to its size. A ₹5,000 gain means nothing until you know whether it came from ₹10,000 or ₹10,00,000. Expressing it as a percentage makes different investments comparable — provided you also compare the same time period." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Return is the number every investment is ultimately judged by. It is also the easiest number to present misleadingly, by choosing a flattering period, ignoring costs, or leaving out income received along the way." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Take the ending value and subtract the beginning value to get the gain or loss.<br><br>2. Add any income received during the period, such as dividends or interest.<br><br>3. Divide by the beginning value to express the result as a proportion.<br><br>4. State the period. A 15% return over one year and 15% over five years are very different outcomes." },
      { t: "formula", title: "The total percentage gain over a holding period, including income", lines: ["<b>Return = (Ending value − Beginning value + Income) ÷ Beginning value</b>", "Ending value = What the investment is worth at the end", "Beginning value = What you paid at the start", "Income = Dividends, interest or other cash received during the period"] },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>You buy a share for ₹500. A year later it is worth ₹540 and it paid a ₹15 dividend.</p><ol><li>Capital gain = ₹540 − ₹500 = ₹40.</li><li>Income = ₹15.</li><li>Total gain = ₹40 + ₹15 = ₹55.</li><li>Return = ₹55 ÷ ₹500 = 0.11 = 11%.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> Ignoring the dividend would have given 8% and understated the result by nearly a third. Any return figure that omits income received is incomplete.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Capital gain", h: "The increase in the price of an asset." },
      { t: "def", term: "Income yield", h: "Cash received during the holding period, as a percentage of the amount invested." },
      { t: "def", term: "Total return", h: "Capital gain plus income together." },
      { t: "def", term: "Annualised return", h: "A multi-period return restated as an equivalent yearly rate." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> Buy at ₹500; a year later the share is ₹540 and paid ₹15 of dividends. Total return?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">(540 − 500 + 15) ÷ 500 = 11%. The price-only answer of 8% misses over a quarter of the result.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> Fund A returned 15% over one year. Fund B returned 15% over five years. Roughly annualise B and compare.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">B's annual rate ≈ 1.15^(1/5) − 1 ≈ 2.8% a year — nowhere near A. Returns without periods are meaningless.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> Your investment shows a 12% return before a 1.5% fee and 10% tax on gains. Estimate what you keep.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">After fee ≈ 10.5%; after 10% tax on that gain ≈ 9.45%. Costs and taxes compound against you every year.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Return is a gain expressed relative to the amount invested.<br>• Total return includes income, not just price change.<br>• A return figure is meaningless without its time period." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Quoting a price gain and calling it the return.<br><br>Comparing returns over different holding periods without annualising.<br><br>Ignoring fees, costs and taxes, which all reduce what you actually keep." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Total return includes…", opts: ["Price change only", "Income only", "Price change plus income received", "Only realised gains"], correct: 2, why: ["Not this one. Dividends and interest are part of the result. Return = (end − start + income) ÷ start.", "Not this one. Dividends and interest are part of the result. Return = (end − start + income) ÷ start.", "Correct. Dividends and interest are part of the result. Return = (end − start + income) ÷ start.", "Not this one. Dividends and interest are part of the result. Return = (end − start + income) ÷ start."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Buy ₹200, sell ₹230, dividends ₹10. The return is…", opts: ["15%", "20%", "10%", "5%"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. (230 − 200 + 10) ÷ 200 = 40 ÷ 200 = 20%.", "Correct. (230 − 200 + 10) ÷ 200 = 40 ÷ 200 = 20%.", "Not this one. (230 − 200 + 10) ÷ 200 = 40 ÷ 200 = 20%.", "Not this one. (230 − 200 + 10) ÷ 200 = 40 ÷ 200 = 20%."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "'This fund made 30%.' The essential missing fact is…", opts: ["The manager's name", "The time period", "The fund's colour scheme", "The office address"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. 30% in one year and 30% over six years are utterly different outcomes. A return without a period is an advertisement, not a measurement.", "Correct. 30% in one year and 30% over six years are utterly different outcomes. A return without a period is an advertisement, not a measurement.", "Not this one. 30% in one year and 30% over six years are utterly different outcomes. A return without a period is an advertisement, not a measurement.", "Not this one. 30% in one year and 30% over six years are utterly different outcomes. A return without a period is an advertisement, not a measurement."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-time-value-of-money"] = {
    id: "c-time-value-of-money", minutes: 5,
    title: "Time value of money",
    short: "Time value of money",
    desc: "₹100 today is worth more than ₹100 next year, and the gap can be calculated exactly.",
    lede: "Money available now is worth more than the same amount later.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Money available now is worth more than the same amount later. There are three reasons: you could invest it and earn a return, prices generally rise so it buys less later, and a future promise might not be kept. This single idea — that timing changes value — is the foundation of essentially all valuation." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Financial decisions almost always compare amounts arriving at different times. Without a way to move money between dates, you simply cannot compare ₹1 crore today against ₹1.5 crore in five years. Time value of money is that tool." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Choose a rate that reflects what you could earn elsewhere at comparable risk.<br><br>2. To move money forward in time, multiply by (1 + rate) for each period. This is compounding.<br><br>3. To move money backward in time, divide by (1 + rate) for each period. This is discounting.<br><br>4. Once every amount has been moved to the same date, you can compare them directly." },
      { t: "formula", title: "The value of an amount at a different point in time", lines: ["<b>FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ   and   PV = FV ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ</b>", "PV = Present value — the amount valued today", "FV = Future value — the amount valued at a future date", "r = The rate per period, as a decimal", "n = The number of periods"] },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>Someone offers you ₹1,50,000 in five years. You could otherwise earn 10% a year. Is it worth ₹1,00,000 today?</p><ol><li>Move the future amount back five years: (1.10)⁵ = 1.6105.</li><li>PV = ₹1,50,000 ÷ 1.6105 = ₹93,140.</li><li>₹93,140 is less than the ₹1,00,000 you would pay.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> You would be paying ₹1,00,000 for something worth ₹93,140 today, so you should decline. Equivalently: ₹1,00,000 invested at 10% grows to ₹1,61,051 in five years, comfortably more than ₹1,50,000.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Present value", h: "What a future amount is worth today." },
      { t: "def", term: "Future value", h: "What a present amount grows to by a future date." },
      { t: "def", term: "Discounting", h: "Moving a future amount back to today's value." },
      { t: "def", term: "Discount rate", h: "The rate used to move money between dates." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> You are offered ₹1,50,000 in five years for ₹1,00,000 today. You can earn 10% elsewhere. Decide, with the calculation.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">PV = 1,50,000 ÷ 1.10⁵ = ₹93,140 < ₹1,00,000. Decline — equivalently, ₹1,00,000 at 10% grows to ₹1,61,051, beating the offer.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> At 8%, would you rather have ₹1,00,000 now or ₹1,25,000 in three years?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">₹1,00,000 → 1,00,000 × 1.08³ = ₹1,25,971. Marginally better to take the money now — and certain.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> Why does adding up ₹50,000-a-year cash flows for 10 years and calling it 'worth ₹5,00,000' overstate the value?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Later rupees are worth less than current ones. Each year's ₹50,000 must be discounted before summing; the undiscounted total ignores time entirely.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Timing changes value, and the change can be calculated precisely.<br>• Compounding moves money forward; discounting moves it back.<br>• Comparisons are only valid once all amounts sit on the same date." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Adding up cash flows from different years without discounting them.<br><br>Mixing an annual rate with monthly periods.<br><br>Using the same discount rate for a safe cash flow and a risky one." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "₹100 today beats ₹100 next year because…", opts: ["Banks are unreliable", "It can be invested, prices rise, and promises can fail", "Cash feels nicer", "Of exchange rates"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Earning power, inflation and uncertainty are the three reasons timing changes value.", "Correct. Earning power, inflation and uncertainty are the three reasons timing changes value.", "Not this one. Earning power, inflation and uncertainty are the three reasons timing changes value.", "Not this one. Earning power, inflation and uncertainty are the three reasons timing changes value."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Moving a future amount back to today's value is called…", opts: ["Compounding", "Discounting", "Amortising", "Netting"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Discounting divides by (1+r) per period; compounding multiplies. They are mirror operations.", "Correct. Discounting divides by (1+r) per period; compounding multiplies. They are mirror operations.", "Not this one. Discounting divides by (1+r) per period; compounding multiplies. They are mirror operations.", "Not this one. Discounting divides by (1+r) per period; compounding multiplies. They are mirror operations."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "At 10%, ₹121 arriving in two years is worth today…", opts: ["₹121", "₹110", "₹100", "₹99"], correct: 2, why: ["Not this one. 121 ÷ 1.10² = 121 ÷ 1.21 = ₹100.", "Not this one. 121 ÷ 1.10² = 121 ÷ 1.21 = ₹100.", "Correct. 121 ÷ 1.10² = 121 ÷ 1.21 = ₹100.", "Not this one. 121 ÷ 1.10² = 121 ÷ 1.21 = ₹100."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-compounding"] = {
    id: "c-compounding", minutes: 5,
    title: "Compounding",
    short: "Compounding",
    desc: "Earning returns on your previous returns, which makes growth accelerate over time.",
    lede: "Compounding is what happens when the returns you earn start earning returns themselves.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Compounding is what happens when the returns you earn start earning returns themselves. In year one you earn on your original money. In year two you earn on the original money plus year one's gain. The base keeps growing, so each year adds more than the last — growth curves upward instead of running in a straight line." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Compounding explains why small differences in rate or time produce enormous differences in outcome, and why starting early matters more than most people expect. It also works against you: it is exactly why unpaid credit-card balances become unmanageable." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Apply the rate to the current balance, not the original amount.<br><br>2. Add the result to the balance.<br><br>3. Repeat. Each cycle starts from a larger base than the last.<br><br>4. The more periods you run, the more the curve pulls away from a straight line." },
      { t: "formula", title: "What an amount grows to when returns are reinvested", lines: ["<b>FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ</b>", "PV = The amount you start with", "r = The return per period, as a decimal", "n = The number of periods"] },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>₹1,00,000 invested at 12% a year.</p><ol><li>After 1 year: ₹1,00,000 × 1.12 = ₹1,12,000.</li><li>After 5 years: ₹1,00,000 × 1.12⁵ = ₹1,76,234.</li><li>After 10 years: ₹1,00,000 × 1.12¹⁰ = ₹3,10,585.</li><li>Simple interest at 12% would have given only ₹2,20,000 after 10 years.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> The first five years added ₹76,234. The next five added ₹1,34,351 — nearly twice as much, from the same rate. Nothing changed except that the base was larger. This is why time in the market matters so much.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Compounding period", h: "How often returns are added to the balance." },
      { t: "def", term: "CAGR", h: "Compound annual growth rate — the constant yearly rate that links a start and end value." },
      { t: "def", term: "Reinvestment", h: "Putting returns back to work rather than withdrawing them." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> ₹1,00,000 at 12% for 10 years. Compute the value after 1, 5 and 10 years, and compare the gain in the first five years with the second five.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">₹1,12,000 · ₹1,76,234 · ₹3,10,585. First five years: +₹76,234. Second five: +₹1,34,351 — almost double, purely because the base grew.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> Approximately how long does money double at 12% compounded annually?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">1.12ⁿ = 2 → n ≈ 6.1 years. (The rule of 72 gives 72 ÷ 12 = 6.)</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> A portfolio falls 50% one year and rises 50% the next. Where does it end up?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">1.00 × 0.50 × 1.50 = 0.75 — down 25%. Losses compound too, and a −50% needs +100% to recover.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Compounding means earning on your earnings, so growth accelerates.<br>• Time matters more than most beginners expect, because the later years contribute most.<br>• The same mechanism makes unpaid high-interest debt grow dangerously fast." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Assuming a 12% return doubles money in roughly 8 years by simple multiplication rather than compounding (it takes about 6).<br><br>Withdrawing returns and still expecting compounded growth.<br><br>Applying an average annual return to a volatile series — losses compound too, and a −50% year needs +100% to recover." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Compounding means…", opts: ["Earning only on the original principal", "Earning returns on previously earned returns", "Adding fixed amounts yearly", "Reducing risk over time"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Each period's gain joins the base, so the base — and each subsequent gain — keeps growing.", "Correct. Each period's gain joins the base, so the base — and each subsequent gain — keeps growing.", "Not this one. Each period's gain joins the base, so the base — and each subsequent gain — keeps growing.", "Not this one. Each period's gain joins the base, so the base — and each subsequent gain — keeps growing."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "₹10,000 at 10% annual compounding after two years is…", opts: ["₹12,000", "₹12,100", "₹11,000", "₹12,500"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. 10,000 × 1.10² = ₹12,100. The extra ₹100 over simple interest is interest on year one's interest.", "Correct. 10,000 × 1.10² = ₹12,100. The extra ₹100 over simple interest is interest on year one's interest.", "Not this one. 10,000 × 1.10² = ₹12,100. The extra ₹100 over simple interest is interest on year one's interest.", "Not this one. 10,000 × 1.10² = ₹12,100. The extra ₹100 over simple interest is interest on year one's interest."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Why does starting to invest 10 years earlier matter so much?", opts: ["Fees fall over time", "The largest gains come in the later years, and early money reaches them", "Markets only rise early", "Taxes disappear"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Growth accelerates as the base builds, so the final years contribute most — and only money invested early experiences them.", "Correct. Growth accelerates as the base builds, so the final years contribute most — and only money invested early experiences them.", "Not this one. Growth accelerates as the base builds, so the final years contribute most — and only money invested early experiences them.", "Not this one. Growth accelerates as the base builds, so the final years contribute most — and only money invested early experiences them."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-inflation"] = {
    id: "c-inflation", minutes: 5,
    title: "Inflation",
    short: "Inflation",
    desc: "Prices rising over time, which quietly reduces what your money can buy.",
    lede: "Inflation is a general rise in prices across the economy.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Inflation is a general rise in prices across the economy. Its practical effect is that a fixed amount of money buys less each year. Your bank balance does not shrink — its purchasing power does. This is why a return that looks positive can still leave you worse off." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Every long-term financial plan must be stated in terms of what money can buy, not how many rupees you hold. A pension that looks generous in today's rupees may be inadequate in thirty years." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Prices across a basket of goods are measured over time to produce an inflation rate.<br><br>2. Nominal amounts are the rupee figures you actually see.<br><br>3. Real amounts adjust those figures for the change in purchasing power.<br><br>4. Roughly, real return ≈ nominal return − inflation. This approximation is fine at low rates." },
      { t: "formula", title: "Return after adjusting for the loss of purchasing power", lines: ["<b>Real return ≈ Nominal return − Inflation rate</b>", "Nominal return = The return in rupee terms, as reported", "Inflation rate = The rate at which prices are rising"] },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>You keep ₹5,00,000 in a savings account paying 4%. Inflation runs at 6%.</p><ol><li>After a year you have ₹5,00,000 × 1.04 = ₹5,20,000 — more rupees than before.</li><li>But the goods that cost ₹5,00,000 last year now cost ₹5,00,000 × 1.06 = ₹5,30,000.</li><li>Real return ≈ 4% − 6% = −2%.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> Your balance grew and your buying power fell. 'Safe' cash is not risk-free — it carries the near-certainty of losing purchasing power when inflation exceeds the interest rate.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Nominal", h: "Stated in current rupees, without adjusting for inflation." },
      { t: "def", term: "Real", h: "Adjusted for inflation, so it reflects purchasing power." },
      { t: "def", term: "Purchasing power", h: "The quantity of goods a given amount of money can buy." },
      { t: "def", term: "Deflation", h: "A general fall in prices — the opposite of inflation." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> ₹5,00,000 sits in savings at 4% while inflation runs 6%. After one year, state the balance and what it can buy relative to today.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Balance ₹5,20,000; last year's ₹5,00,000 basket now costs ₹5,30,000. Real return ≈ −2%: more rupees, less purchasing power.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> Precisely, what is the real return when nominal is 9% and inflation is 5%?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">(1.09 ÷ 1.05) − 1 = 3.81%. The subtraction shortcut (4%) is close at low rates but not exact.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> You want the purchasing power of today's ₹50,000/month in 20 years with 5% inflation. What nominal amount is that?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">50,000 × 1.05²⁰ ≈ ₹1,32,665 per month. Plans stated in today's rupees quietly shrink.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Inflation reduces what money buys, even when the rupee amount rises.<br>• Real return is the figure that reflects whether you are actually better off.<br>• Never mix nominal cash flows with a real rate, or the reverse, in the same calculation." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Treating cash as risk-free when inflation exceeds the interest earned.<br><br>Building a 30-year plan in today's rupees without inflating the target.<br><br>Mistaking one product becoming expensive for economy-wide inflation." },
      { t: "where", h: "Inflation rates, the measurement basket and the official index differ by country and are revised over time. Treat any specific rate as a current observation, not a fixed fact." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Inflation primarily erodes…", opts: ["The number of rupees you hold", "What your rupees can buy", "Bank security", "Interest rates"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Balances do not shrink; purchasing power does. That is why real, not nominal, figures measure whether you gained.", "Correct. Balances do not shrink; purchasing power does. That is why real, not nominal, figures measure whether you gained.", "Not this one. Balances do not shrink; purchasing power does. That is why real, not nominal, figures measure whether you gained.", "Not this one. Balances do not shrink; purchasing power does. That is why real, not nominal, figures measure whether you gained."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Nominal return 4%, inflation 6%. Approximately, your real return is…", opts: ["+2%", "0%", "−2%", "+10%"], correct: 2, why: ["Not this one. Real ≈ nominal − inflation = −2%. 'Safe' cash loses purchasing power whenever inflation outruns interest.", "Not this one. Real ≈ nominal − inflation = −2%. 'Safe' cash loses purchasing power whenever inflation outruns interest.", "Correct. Real ≈ nominal − inflation = −2%. 'Safe' cash loses purchasing power whenever inflation outruns interest.", "Not this one. Real ≈ nominal − inflation = −2%. 'Safe' cash loses purchasing power whenever inflation outruns interest."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "A consistent valuation must…", opts: ["Mix real cash flows with nominal rates", "Use nominal with nominal, or real with real", "Ignore inflation entirely", "Always use real terms"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Mixing conventions double-counts or ignores inflation. Either frame works — mixed frames do not.", "Correct. Mixing conventions double-counts or ignores inflation. Either frame works — mixed frames do not.", "Not this one. Mixing conventions double-counts or ignores inflation. Either frame works — mixed frames do not.", "Not this one. Mixing conventions double-counts or ignores inflation. Either frame works — mixed frames do not."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-present-value"] = {
    id: "c-present-value", minutes: 5,
    title: "Present value",
    short: "Present value",
    desc: "What a future amount of money is worth today.",
    lede: "Present value answers one question: if someone will pay me a certain amount on a future date, how much is that promise worth right now? You find it by discounting — dividing the future amount by the growth you could otherwise have achieved over that time..",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Present value answers one question: if someone will pay me a certain amount on a future date, how much is that promise worth right now? You find it by discounting — dividing the future amount by the growth you could otherwise have achieved over that time." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Present value is the engine inside every valuation method you will meet later. Discounted cash flow analysis, bond pricing and project appraisal are all repeated applications of this one calculation." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Identify each future cash flow and the exact date it arrives.<br><br>2. Choose a discount rate reflecting the return available elsewhere at similar risk.<br><br>3. Divide each cash flow by (1 + r) raised to the number of periods until it arrives.<br><br>4. Add the discounted amounts together to get the total present value." },
      { t: "formula", title: "Today's value of a single future cash flow", lines: ["<b>PV = CF ÷ (1 + r)ᵗ</b>", "CF = The cash flow arriving in the future", "r = The discount rate per period", "t = Number of periods until the cash flow arrives"] },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>A project pays ₹50,000 at the end of year 1 and ₹80,000 at the end of year 2. Your discount rate is 10%.</p><ol><li>Year 1: ₹50,000 ÷ 1.10 = ₹45,455.</li><li>Year 2: ₹80,000 ÷ (1.10)² = ₹80,000 ÷ 1.21 = ₹66,116.</li><li>Total present value = ₹45,455 + ₹66,116 = ₹1,11,571.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> The project's promised cash adds up to ₹1,30,000, but it is worth ₹1,11,571 today. If it costs less than ₹1,11,571 it adds value; if it costs more, it does not.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Discounting", h: "Converting a future amount into today's value." },
      { t: "def", term: "Discount rate", h: "The rate used to discount, reflecting time and risk." },
      { t: "def", term: "Net present value", h: "Total present value of inflows minus the upfront cost." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> A project pays ₹50,000 in year 1 and ₹80,000 in year 2; your rate is 10%. Value it.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">50,000÷1.10 = ₹45,455; 80,000÷1.21 = ₹66,116. Total PV = ₹1,11,571.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> The same project costs ₹1,05,000 upfront. Take it?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Yes: net present value = 1,11,571 − 1,05,000 = +₹6,571. It creates value at a 10% opportunity cost.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> Recompute the PV at 15% and explain the direction of the change.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">50,000÷1.15 + 80,000÷1.3225 = 43,478 + 60,491 = ₹1,03,969. A higher rate means future money is worth less today — now below the ₹1,05,000 cost.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Present value converts future money into today's terms so amounts can be compared.<br>• A higher discount rate produces a lower present value.<br>• Cash flows further in the future are discounted more heavily." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Getting the number of periods wrong by one — check whether a cash flow arrives at the start or end of a year.<br><br>Using one discount rate for cash flows of very different risk.<br><br>Summing undiscounted cash flows and calling the total a value." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "PV of ₹1,10,000 arriving in one year at 10% is…", opts: ["₹1,10,000", "₹1,00,000", "₹99,000", "₹1,21,000"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. 1,10,000 ÷ 1.10 = ₹1,00,000.", "Correct. 1,10,000 ÷ 1.10 = ₹1,00,000.", "Not this one. 1,10,000 ÷ 1.10 = ₹1,00,000.", "Not this one. 1,10,000 ÷ 1.10 = ₹1,00,000."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "When the discount rate rises, present values…", opts: ["Rise", "Fall", "Stay fixed", "Turn negative"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. You are dividing by a larger factor. This single mechanic is why valuations fall when required returns rise.", "Correct. You are dividing by a larger factor. This single mechanic is why valuations fall when required returns rise.", "Not this one. You are dividing by a larger factor. This single mechanic is why valuations fall when required returns rise.", "Not this one. You are dividing by a larger factor. This single mechanic is why valuations fall when required returns rise."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Cash flows arriving further in the future are…", opts: ["Discounted more heavily", "Discounted less", "Never discounted", "Added at face value"], correct: 0, why: ["Correct. The divisor (1+r)ᵗ grows with t, so distant money contributes less to today's value.", "Not this one. The divisor (1+r)ᵗ grows with t, so distant money contributes less to today's value.", "Not this one. The divisor (1+r)ᵗ grows with t, so distant money contributes less to today's value.", "Not this one. The divisor (1+r)ᵗ grows with t, so distant money contributes less to today's value."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-future-value"] = {
    id: "c-future-value", minutes: 5,
    title: "Future value",
    short: "Future value",
    desc: "What an amount you hold today will grow to by a future date.",
    lede: "Future value is the mirror image of present value.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Future value is the mirror image of present value. Instead of asking what a future amount is worth today, you ask what today's amount becomes later. You multiply by (1 + rate) once for each period the money stays invested." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Future value is how you set and test savings goals. It converts a plan — 'I will save this much at this rate for this long' — into a number you can check against what you actually need." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Start with the amount you have today.<br><br>2. Multiply by (1 + r) for each period the money remains invested.<br><br>3. If you add money regularly, each contribution compounds only for the periods remaining after it arrives.<br><br>4. Small changes in rate or in the number of years change the answer a great deal." },
      { t: "formula", title: "What a present amount grows to by a future date", lines: ["<b>FV = PV × (1 + r)ⁿ</b>", "PV = The amount today", "r = The rate per period, as a decimal", "n = The number of periods"] },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>You invest ₹2,00,000 today at 9% a year and leave it for 12 years.</p><ol><li>Growth factor = (1.09)¹² = 2.8127.</li><li>FV = ₹2,00,000 × 2.8127 = ₹5,62,540.</li><li>At 11% instead: (1.11)¹² = 3.4985, so FV = ₹6,99,700.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> Two extra percentage points added ₹1,37,160 — about 68% of the original investment — without you saving a rupee more. Rate and time do the heavy lifting in long-horizon saving.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Growth factor", h: "The multiple (1 + r)ⁿ by which an amount grows." },
      { t: "def", term: "Horizon", h: "How long money stays invested." },
      { t: "def", term: "Regular contribution", h: "An amount added each period, each compounding for the time remaining." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> ₹2,00,000 invested for 12 years at 9%. Future value?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">2,00,000 × 1.09¹² = 2,00,000 × 2.8127 = ₹5,62,540.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> Same money at 11% instead. How much extra do two percentage points deliver?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">2,00,000 × 1.11¹² = ₹6,99,700 — an extra ₹1,37,160, about 68% of the original sum, from rate alone.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> You need ₹10,00,000 in 8 years and can earn 10%. How much must you invest today?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">This is FV in reverse: 10,00,000 ÷ 1.10⁸ = ₹4,66,507.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Future value moves today's money forward to a future date.<br>• Small rate differences compound into large amounts over long horizons.<br>• Money added later compounds for less time and contributes proportionally less." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Applying an annual rate to monthly contributions without converting the rate.<br><br>Assuming a projected future value is a promise rather than a projection.<br><br>Forgetting that inflation will reduce what that future amount buys." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "FV of ₹50,000 at 8% for 3 years is closest to…", opts: ["₹62,000", "₹62,986", "₹58,000", "₹54,000"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. 50,000 × 1.08³ = 50,000 × 1.2597 = ₹62,986.", "Correct. 50,000 × 1.08³ = 50,000 × 1.2597 = ₹62,986.", "Not this one. 50,000 × 1.08³ = 50,000 × 1.2597 = ₹62,986.", "Not this one. 50,000 × 1.08³ = 50,000 × 1.2597 = ₹62,986."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Doubling the number of years (at the same rate)…", opts: ["Doubles the future value", "More than doubles the growth, because compounding accelerates", "Halves it", "Has no effect"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Growth is exponential: (1+r)²ⁿ = ((1+r)ⁿ)². The second half of the horizon adds more than the first.", "Correct. Growth is exponential: (1+r)²ⁿ = ((1+r)ⁿ)². The second half of the horizon adds more than the first.", "Not this one. Growth is exponential: (1+r)²ⁿ = ((1+r)ⁿ)². The second half of the horizon adds more than the first.", "Not this one. Growth is exponential: (1+r)²ⁿ = ((1+r)ⁿ)². The second half of the horizon adds more than the first."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "A projected future value of your investment plan is…", opts: ["A guarantee", "A projection that depends on the assumed rate holding", "A legal contract", "Inflation-proof"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Change the assumed rate and the answer changes materially. FV outputs are only as good as their inputs.", "Correct. Change the assumed rate and the answer changes materially. FV outputs are only as good as their inputs.", "Not this one. Change the assumed rate and the answer changes materially. FV outputs are only as good as their inputs.", "Not this one. Change the assumed rate and the answer changes materially. FV outputs are only as good as their inputs."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-opportunity-cost"] = {
    id: "c-opportunity-cost", minutes: 5,
    title: "Opportunity cost",
    short: "Opportunity cost",
    desc: "The value of the best thing you gave up in order to do what you chose.",
    lede: "Every choice rules out other choices.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Every choice rules out other choices. Opportunity cost is the value of the best alternative you did not take. It rarely appears in accounts or on a bank statement, but it is often the most important number in a decision, because a choice can be profitable and still be the wrong one." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "Opportunity cost is where discount rates come from. When you ask what rate to use in a present value calculation, you are asking what you could earn on the next-best use of that money at similar risk." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. List the realistic alternatives, not just the one being proposed.<br><br>2. Estimate the return of the best alternative at comparable risk.<br><br>3. Judge the proposed option against that alternative, not against zero.<br><br>4. Ignore money already spent that you cannot recover — it is the same under every option." },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>You have ₹5,00,000. You can put it into a friend's business expecting 11%, or repay a personal loan charging 14%.</p><ol><li>Business: expected gain ≈ ₹5,00,000 × 11% = ₹55,000, and uncertain.</li><li>Loan repayment: interest avoided = ₹5,00,000 × 14% = ₹70,000, and certain.</li><li>Choosing the business costs you ₹70,000 of certain savings to chase ₹55,000 of uncertain gain.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> The business investment is 'profitable' in isolation and still the wrong decision. Repaying expensive debt is a guaranteed 14% return, and almost nothing available to an individual beats a certain 14%.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Opportunity cost", h: "The value of the best alternative forgone." },
      { t: "def", term: "Sunk cost", h: "Money already spent that cannot be recovered and should not influence the decision." },
      { t: "def", term: "Hurdle rate", h: "The minimum return a project must beat, set by the best alternative." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> ₹5,00,000 can go into a friend's venture at an expected 11% or repay your 14% loan. Compute both outcomes and decide.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Venture: ₹55,000, uncertain. Repayment: ₹70,000 saved, certain. Repay — the venture's true cost includes the ₹70,000 forgone.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> You spent ₹3,00,000 developing a product that now needs ₹2,00,000 more, with expected sales of ₹2,50,000. What is relevant to the decision, and what is not?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Relevant: ₹2,00,000 more against ₹2,50,000 expected — proceed if the estimate is believable. Irrelevant: the sunk ₹3,00,000, which no decision can recover.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> Your discount rate for a project 'should reflect opportunity cost'. Say what that means operationally.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">It is the return available on the best alternative of similar risk. A project must beat that alternative, not merely beat zero.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Judge every option against the best alternative, not against doing nothing.<br>• Opportunity cost is where discount rates and hurdle rates come from.<br>• Sunk costs are irrelevant to what you should do next." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Comparing an investment to zero return instead of to the realistic alternative.<br><br>Continuing a failing project because of money already spent.<br><br>Ignoring non-money costs such as your own time." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Opportunity cost is…", opts: ["The cash price of an item", "The value of the best alternative you gave up", "A bank fee", "Always zero for cash purchases"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Every choice forecloses alternatives; the best forgone one is the real benchmark.", "Correct. Every choice forecloses alternatives; the best forgone one is the real benchmark.", "Not this one. Every choice forecloses alternatives; the best forgone one is the real benchmark.", "Not this one. Every choice forecloses alternatives; the best forgone one is the real benchmark."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Money already spent that cannot be recovered is called…", opts: ["Fixed cost", "Sunk cost", "Opportunity cost", "Marginal cost"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Sunk costs are identical under every option, so they should not steer the next decision — though emotionally they often do.", "Correct. Sunk costs are identical under every option, so they should not steer the next decision — though emotionally they often do.", "Not this one. Sunk costs are identical under every option, so they should not steer the next decision — though emotionally they often do.", "Not this one. Sunk costs are identical under every option, so they should not steer the next decision — though emotionally they often do."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "An investment returning 9% when your best comparable alternative returns 12% is…", opts: ["Profitable and correct", "Profitable but the wrong choice", "A loss-maker", "Risk-free"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. It earns money yet destroys ₹3 per ₹100 per year relative to the alternative. Judged against the right benchmark, it fails.", "Correct. It earns money yet destroys ₹3 per ₹100 per year relative to the alternative. Judged against the right benchmark, it fails.", "Not this one. It earns money yet destroys ₹3 per ₹100 per year relative to the alternative. Judged against the right benchmark, it fails.", "Not this one. It earns money yet destroys ₹3 per ₹100 per year relative to the alternative. Judged against the right benchmark, it fails."] }
    ]
  };

  LS.lessons["c-risk-vs-reward"] = {
    id: "c-risk-vs-reward", minutes: 5,
    title: "Risk vs reward",
    short: "Risk vs reward",
    desc: "Higher expected returns exist because higher risks are being accepted — never as a free gift.",
    lede: "In a functioning market, you are not offered a higher expected return for nothing.",
    body: [
      { t: "h2", text: "What is this?" },
      { t: "p", h: "In a functioning market, you are not offered a higher expected return for nothing. If an investment promises more, it is because something about it is less certain: the borrower might not pay, the price might swing, or you might not be able to sell when you want. The trade-off is the organising principle of investing." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Why does it matter?" },
      { t: "p", h: "This idea is your best protection against both fraud and self-deception. When something offers high returns with no apparent risk, the correct response is to find the risk — not to assume it does not exist." },
      { t: "h2", text: "How does it work?" },
      { t: "p", h: "1. Start with the return on the safest available asset — typically short-term government debt.<br><br>2. Every riskier option must offer more than this to attract money at all.<br><br>3. The extra return above the safe rate is called the risk premium.<br><br>4. Your job as an investor is to judge whether the premium is large enough for the risk you are taking." },
      { t: "formula", title: "The expected return required for a risky investment", lines: ["<b>Expected return = Risk-free rate + Risk premium</b>", "Risk-free rate = The return on the safest available asset", "Risk premium = The extra expected return demanded for bearing the risk"] },
      { t: "example", h: "<p>Government bonds yield 7%. Three options are offered to you.</p><ol><li>A large stable company's bond yields 8.5%. Premium = 1.5% for the chance it cannot repay.</li><li>A diversified equity portfolio has expected return around 12%. Premium = 5% for price swings and uncertain profits.</li><li>A scheme promises a guaranteed 24% with 'no risk'. Premium = 17% for a risk the promoter says does not exist.</li></ol><p><strong>What it means.</strong> The first two premiums are explainable — you can name what you are being paid to bear. The third cannot be. A 17% premium with no identifiable risk means the risk has been hidden, not removed.</p>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "Key terms" },
      { t: "def", term: "Risk-free rate", h: "The return on the safest available asset, usually short-term government debt." },
      { t: "def", term: "Risk premium", h: "Extra expected return demanded for accepting risk." },
      { t: "def", term: "Expected return", h: "The probability-weighted average of possible outcomes — not a promise." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Practice" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 1.</strong> Government bonds pay 7%. A corporate bond pays 8.5% and a diversified equity portfolio is expected around 12%. Name each risk premium and what it pays for.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">Corporate: 1.5% for default risk. Equity: 5% for uncertain profits and price swings. Each extra point maps to a nameable risk.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 2.</strong> A scheme guarantees 24% with 'zero risk' while the safe rate is 7%. Apply the framework.<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">A 17-point premium must be payment for some risk. If none is disclosed, the risk is concealed — commonly the risk that the promoter simply keeps your money.</p></details>" },
      { t: "note", h: "<strong>Exercise 3.</strong> Why is an 'expected return of 12%' not a promise of 12%?<details style=\"margin-top:.6rem\"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p style=\"margin-top:.5rem\">It is the probability-weighted average of many outcomes — some far better, some negative. You experience one outcome, never the average.</p></details>" },
      { t: "h2", text: "What to remember" },
      { t: "note", h: "Higher expected return is compensation for risk, never a free gift.<br>• If you cannot name the risk you are being paid for, you have not found it yet.<br>• 'Guaranteed' and 'high return' together should always trigger scrutiny." },
      { t: "h2", text: "Common mistakes" },
      { t: "p", h: "Reading an expected return as a promised return.<br><br>Assuming an investment that has not lost money yet is low risk.<br><br>Comparing returns across options without comparing their risks." },
      { t: "mcq", q: "The extra return above the risk-free rate is called…", opts: ["Bonus yield", "The risk premium", "Alpha", "Spread income"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Every risky asset must offer a premium over the safe rate to attract money; sizing it is the investor's core judgement.", "Correct. Every risky asset must offer a premium over the safe rate to attract money; sizing it is the investor's core judgement.", "Not this one. Every risky asset must offer a premium over the safe rate to attract money; sizing it is the investor's core judgement.", "Not this one. Every risky asset must offer a premium over the safe rate to attract money; sizing it is the investor's core judgement."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "The healthiest response to 'high returns, no risk' is…", opts: ["Invest quickly before it closes", "Ask what risk the premium is paying for", "Assume regulators checked it", "Tell friends"], correct: 1, why: ["Not this one. Premiums are compensation, never gifts. Unfindable risk means hidden risk.", "Correct. Premiums are compensation, never gifts. Unfindable risk means hidden risk.", "Not this one. Premiums are compensation, never gifts. Unfindable risk means hidden risk.", "Not this one. Premiums are compensation, never gifts. Unfindable risk means hidden risk."] },
      { t: "mcq", q: "Safe rate 7%, an asset's risk premium 5%. Its required return is…", opts: ["7%", "5%", "12%", "35%"], correct: 2, why: ["Not this one. Required return = risk-free rate + premium = 12%. Pay a price implying less and you are underpaid for the risk.", "Not this one. Required return = risk-free rate + premium = 12%. Pay a price implying less and you are underpaid for the risk.", "Correct. Required return = risk-free rate + premium = 12%. Pay a price implying less and you are underpaid for the risk.", "Not this one. Required return = risk-free rate + premium = 12%. Pay a price implying less and you are underpaid for the risk."] }
    ]
  };
